    "answer": "### INDICATORS OF HEALTH\nHealth indicators are variables that help to measure changes, directly or indirectly, in health status and the effectiveness of health services, and to assess health goals. Because health cannot be measured directly in quantitative terms, we rely on these proxy indicators.\n\n#### 1. Characteristics of an Ideal Health Indicator (R-V-S-S-O-F)\n*   **Validity**: It must measure what it is intended to measure (e.g., IMR reflecting infant mortality and infant care quality).\n*   **Reliability**: It must yield identical results when repeated by different observers under similar circumstances.\n*   **Sensitivity**: It should be sensitive to changes in the situation being observed.\n*   **Specificity**: It must reflect changes only in the specific situation.\n*   **Feasibility**: The data required should be easily, cheaply, and reliably collectable.\n*   **Relevance**: It should be relevant to community needs and national health policies.\n\n#### 2. Comprehensive Classification of Health Indicators\n1.  **Mortality Indicators**: Reflected by frequency of deaths.\n    *   *Crude Death Rate (CDR)*: Total deaths per 1,000 population per year.\n    *   *Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)*: Deaths of infants under 1 year per 1,000 live births. It is the most sensitive indicator of health, sanitation, and socio-economic status.\n    *   *Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)*: Maternal deaths per 100,000 live births due to pregnancy-related causes.\n    *   *Expectation of Life (Life Expectancy)*: The average number of years a newborn is expected to live if current mortality trends continue.\n    *   *Under-5 Mortality Rate*: Deaths of children under 5 years per 1,000 live births.\n    *   *Disease-Specific Death Rates*: Deaths due to a specific disease per 100,000 population.\n2.  **Morbidity Indicators**: Measure the frequency and severity of disease.\n    *   *Incidence Rate*: Number of new cases occurring in a defined time.\n    *   *Prevalence Rate*: Total number of cases (old + new) at a point in time or period.\n    *   *Notification rates, hospital attendance, admission, and discharge rates*.\n3.  **Disability Rates**:\n    *   *Sullivan's Index*: Disability-free life expectancy (Life Expectancy minus years of bed-disability or restricted activity).\n    *   *Disability-Adjusted Life Years (DALY)*: Measures the burden of disease. One DALY equals one lost year of healthy life. Computed as: $\\text{DALY} = \\text{YLL} (\\text{Years of Life Lost}) + \\text{YLD} (\\text{Years Lived with Disability})$.\n    *   *Quality-Adjusted Life Years (QALY)*: Combines quantity and quality of survival.\n4.  **Nutritional Status Indicators**:\n    *   Prevalence of low birth weight (<2.5 kg).\n    *   Anthropometric measures: Height-for-age (stunting), weight-for-height (wasting), and mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC).\n5.  **Health Care Delivery Indicators**:\n    *   Doctor-population ratio.\n    *   Nurse-population ratio.\n    *   Bed-population ratio.\n6.  **Utilization Rates**: Expresses the percentage of the population using available services.\n    *   *Immunization coverage*: Proportion of children fully immunized.\n    *   *Antenatal Care (ANC) coverage*: Proportion of pregnant women receiving at least 4 ANC checkups.\n    *   *Contraceptive Prevalence Rate (CPR)*.\n7.  **Indicators of Social and Mental Health**:\n    *   Rates of suicide, homicide, alcohol/drug abuse, crime, and divorce.\n8.  **Environmental Indicators**:\n    *   Proportion of population with access to safe drinking water and sanitation.\n    *   Air quality index (PM2.5, PM10) and radiation levels.\n9.  **Socio-economic Indicators**:\n    *   Gross National Product (GNP) per capita.\n    *   Adult literacy rate (especially female literacy).\n    *   Dependency ratio and unemployment rate.\n10. **Health Policy Indicators**:\n    *   Proportion of Gross Domestic Product (GDP) spent on health services.\n    *   Proportion of national resources allocated to primary health care.",
