    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### THE ICEBERG PHENOMENON OF DISEASE\nUsed in epidemiology to represent the visual distribution of a disease in a community, highlighting the vast difference between visible and hidden morbidity.\n* **The Floating Tip**: Represents the visible portion of disease—diagnosed cases, symptomatic patients, those admitted to hospitals, or deceased cases. This is what the doctor sees in the clinic.\n* **The Submerged Portion**: Represents the massive, invisible portion of disease in the community—subclinical infections, pre-symptomatic cases, healthy carriers, and undiagnosed active cases.\n* **The Water Line**: The boundary between diagnosed and undiagnosed cases (dependent on diagnostic availability and screening).\n* **Epidemiological Importance**: \n  * In many chronic diseases (Hypertension, Diabetes, HIV, Tuberculosis), the submerged portion is vastly larger than the tip. \n  * Screening programs aim to \"raise the water line\" to detect the submerged cases early.\n  * *Note*: Some diseases do not exhibit the iceberg phenomenon (e.g., Tetanus, Rabies, where every infected case is symptomatic/fatal, meaning only the tip exists).",
