    "stars": 6,
    "answer": "### 1. THE EPIDEMIOLOGICAL TRIAD (CLASSICAL MODEL)\nDisease results from the complex interaction between three main factors: the **Agent**, the **Host**, and the **Environment**.\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Agent --> Host\n    Host --> Environment\n    Environment --> Agent\n```\n* **Agent Factors**: The starting point of disease.\n  * *Biological*: Bacteria, viruses, parasites.\n  * *Nutritional*: Deficiencies (proteins, vitamins) or excess.\n  * *Chemical*: Endogenous (urea, uric acid) or exogenous (lead, allergens).\n  * *Physical*: Heat, cold, radiation, noise.\n* **Host Factors**: The human element determining susceptibility and severity.\n  * *Demographic*: Age, sex, ethnicity.\n  * *Biological*: Genetic traits, immune status, physiological states (pregnancy).\n  * *Socio-economic*: Occupation, education, habits (smoking, alcohol).\n* **Environmental Factors**: Everything external to the host.\n  * *Physical*: Air, water, climate, housing.\n  * *Biological*: Vectors, reservoirs of infection.\n  * *Psychosocial*: Poverty, stress, cultural beliefs, healthcare access.\n\n### 2. ADVANCED TRIANGLES OF EPIDEMIOLOGY (MODERN CHRONIC DISEASE MODEL)\nUsed for non-communicable and multi-factorial chronic diseases (e.g., obesity, cardiovascular disease, cancer) where a single biological \"agent\" is absent.\n* **The Four Cornerstones**:\n  1. **Group or Population (Host)**: Demographic characteristics, age profile, genetic susceptibility.\n  2. **Causative Factors (Replaces Agent)**: Complex mix of genetics, behavioral choices (sedentary lifestyle, high-fat diet), environmental exposures, and pathogens.\n  3. **Environment**: Behavioral patterns, sociocultural norms, ecology, and physical surroundings.\n  4. **Time (At the center)**: Time-related factors (latency periods, age of onset, duration of exposure, seasonality).",
