    "chapter": "Concepts of Health and Disease",
    "stars": 2,
    "answer": "### THE WEB OF CAUSATION OF DISEASE\nSuggested by MacMahon, Pugh, and Ipsen, this model explains the etiology of complex, multi-factorial chronic diseases (e.g., coronary heart disease, lung cancer, diabetes) where a single biological agent is absent.\n* **Key Concept**: \n  * Disease does not arise from a single isolated cause, but from a highly interconnected, complex network of multiple risk factors and pathways.\n  * Each factor (lifestyle, genetic susceptibility, environmental exposure, metabolic state) interacts with and reinforces others.\n* **Epidemiological Importance**: \n  * It shifts the focus from looking for a single \"magic bullet\" cure to identifying multiple points of intervention.\n  * Breaking any link in the web (e.g., reducing smoking, lowering blood pressure through diet) can significantly reduce the overall incidence of the disease.",
