    "text": "Add a note on MORBIDITY INDICATORS.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Concepts of Health and Disease",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### MORBIDITY INDICATORS\nMorbidity refers to any departure, subjective or objective, from a state of physiological or psychological well-being. Morbidity indicators are used to measure the frequency, duration, and severity of illness within a community, which is essential for assessing health status and planning health services.\n\n#### 1. Key Morbidity Indicators (Park's Classification)\n1.  **Incidence Rate**:\n    *   *Definition*: The number of **new cases** of a disease occurring in a specified population during a defined period of time.\n    *   *Formula*: $\\text{Incidence} = \\frac{\\text{Number of new cases of specific disease during a given period}}{\\text{Population at risk during that period}} \\times 1000$.\n    *   *Uses*: Useful for acute illnesses (e.g., influenza, food poisoning) and for investigating the etiology or risk factors of diseases.\n2.  **Prevalence Rate**:\n    *   *Definition*: The **total number of cases** (both old and new) of a disease existing in a given population at a specific point in time (Point Prevalence) or over a period of time (Period Prevalence).\n    *   *Formula*: $\\text{Prevalence} = \\frac{\\text{Number of current (new + old) cases at a given time}}{\\text{Estimated population at that time}} \\times 100$.\n    *   *Uses*: Highly useful for chronic diseases (e.g., tuberculosis, diabetes, hypertension) to estimate the community's healthcare burden and plan administrative resources.\n    *   *Relationship*: $\\text{Prevalence (P)} = \\text{Incidence (I)} \\times \\text{Mean Duration of disease (D)}$.\n3.  **Notification Rates**:\n    *   The rate at which legally notifiable diseases (e.g., tuberculosis, cholera, measles) are reported to public health authorities. It helps monitor disease outbreaks and implement control measures.\n4.  **Attendance Rates at Outpatient Departments (OPD)**:\n    *   Reflects the volume of health-seeking behavior in a community, representing the primary workload of outpatient clinics.\n5.  **Admission, Readmission, and Discharge Rates**:\n    *   Hospital-based statistics that indicate the severity of morbidity and the utilization of hospital beds.\n6.  **Duration of Stay in Hospital**:\n    *   The average number of days a patient remains in the hospital. It measures both disease severity and hospital resource utilization.\n7.  **Spells of Sickness or Absence from Work/School**:\n    *   Measures the socio-economic and productivity impact of illness on the working-age population.",
