    "text": "Write about international classification of diseases.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Concepts of Health and Disease",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### INTERNATIONAL CLASSIFICATION OF DISEASES (ICD)\nThe International Classification of Diseases (ICD) is the global standard diagnostic tool for epidemiology, health management, and clinical research. It is maintained and periodically revised by the **World Health Organization (WHO)**.\n\n#### 1. Objectives and Purpose\n*   To translate diagnoses of diseases, injuries, and health problems from words into standardized alphanumeric codes.\n*   To enable systematic storage, retrieval, analysis, and comparison of mortality and morbidity data worldwide.\n*   To facilitate international comparability in health reporting and statistics.\n\n#### 2. Evolution and Coding Structure\n*   Originating from Jacques Bertillon's \"Classification of Causes of Death\" in 1893, the WHO took over its maintenance in 1948 (starting with ICD-6), expanding its scope to include non-fatal diseases (morbidity).\n*   **ICD-10 (10th Revision)**: Uses a 3-character alphanumeric code (a letter followed by two numbers, e.g., **A00** for cholera) that can be further sub-classified into 4-character codes (e.g., **A00.0** for cholera due to *Vibrio cholerae 01*). It contains **21 chapters** grouping diseases by etiology, anatomical site, or clinical circumstances.\n*   **ICD-11 (11th Revision)**: Officially adopted by the World Health Assembly in 2019 and came into effect globally on **January 1, 2022**. It is fully digital, integrates seamlessly with electronic health records (EHRs), and contains new chapters such as traditional medicine, sexual health, and gaming disorder.\n\n#### 3. Core Public Health Applications\n*   *Mortality Statistics*: Standardizing causes of death written on death certificates.\n*   *Health Billing and Reimbursement*: Crucial for healthcare financing and insurance claims.\n*   *Epidemiological Surveillance*: Monitoring disease outbreaks and tracking national/global trends.",
