    "answer": "### REHABILITATION AND ITS TYPES\nRehabilitation is defined as the combined and coordinated use of medical, social, educational, and vocational measures for training or retraining the individual to the highest possible level of functional ability.\n\n#### 1. Level of Prevention\nRehabilitation is the primary component of **Tertiary Prevention**. It aims to minimize the social and occupational impact of irreversible physical or mental damage (disability and handicap) and facilitate integration into the community.\n\n#### 2. Comprehensive Types of Rehabilitation\n1.  **Medical Rehabilitation**:\n    *   *Goal*: Restoring physical and physiological function.\n    *   *Methods*: Physiotherapy (exercise, heat therapy), occupational therapy, reconstructive surgery (e.g., tendon transfer in leprosy, joint replacement in arthritis), and the provision of prosthetics (artificial limbs) or orthotics (braces/splints).\n2.  **Vocational Rehabilitation**:\n    *   *Goal*: Re-establishing economic independence and productivity.\n    *   *Methods*: Assessing vocational skills, providing occupational guidance, training or retraining in new skills suitable to the physical capacity, and facilitating job placement in normal or sheltered workshops.\n3.  **Social Rehabilitation**:\n    *   *Goal*: Restoring family and community relationships.\n    *   *Methods*: Overcoming social stigma, assisting in personal adjustment, modifying home environments to accommodate physical limitations, and improving accessibility in public spaces (e.g., ramps, braille signs).\n4.  **Psychological Rehabilitation**:\n    *   *Goal*: Restoring self-confidence, dignity, and mental well-being.\n    *   *Methods*: Psychological counseling and cognitive behavioral therapy to manage anxiety, depression, or post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) associated with the loss of physical structure or function.",
