    "text": "Sentinel surveillance",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Concepts of Health and Disease",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### SENTINEL SURVEILLANCE\nSentinel surveillance is a specialized epidemiological method of monitoring key health events or specific diseases through a network of selected, highly standardized reporting sites (sentinel sites).\n* **Mechanism**: Instead of attempting to collect reports of every single case in the entire country (which is extremely expensive and logistically difficult), designated clinics, hospitals, or labs act as \"sentinels\" to systematically collect and report high-quality, detailed data on the disease.\n* **Key Uses**:\n  1. **HIV/AIDS**: Tracking prevalence rates among high-risk groups (truckers, sex workers) and low-risk groups (pregnant women at ANC clinics).\n  2. **Influenza**: Monitoring circulating virus strains during flu seasons.\n* **Advantages**: Highly cost-effective, yields exceptionally reliable and detailed data, and acts as an early warning system for outbreaks.",
