    "text": "Impairment ,disability and Handicap.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Concepts of Health and Disease",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### IMPAIRMENT, DISABILITY, AND HANDICAP\nThe World Health Organization (WHO) established a standardized classification of the consequences of disease and injury to clarify terminology in rehabilitation medicine.\n\n#### 1. Definitions and Levels of Manifestation\n1.  **Impairment**:\n    *   *Definition*: Any loss or abnormality of psychological, physiological, or anatomical structure or function.\n    *   *Level*: **Organ Level**. It represents the objective, biological manifestation of the pathology.\n    *   *Examples*: Blindness (damaged retina/optic nerve), amputation of a leg, complete loss of hearing.\n2.  **Disability**:\n    *   *Definition*: Any restriction or lack (resulting from an impairment) of ability to perform an activity in the manner or within the range considered normal for a human being.\n    *   *Level*: **Personal Level**. It represents the functional limitations experienced by the individual.\n    *   *Examples*: Inability to walk (due to leg amputation), inability to read normal print (due to blindness).\n3.  **Handicap**:\n    *   *Definition*: A disadvantage for a given individual, resulting from an impairment or a disability, that limits or prevents the fulfillment of a role that is normal (depending on age, sex, and social and cultural factors) for that individual.\n    *   *Level*: **Social Level**. It represents the social, economic, or environmental consequences of the impairment/disability.\n    *   *Examples*: Inability to gain employment, social isolation, loss of social status.\n\n#### 2. Sequential Relationship (Linear Model)\n$$\\text{Disease/Injury} \\rightarrow \\text{Impairment (Organ Level)} \\rightarrow \\text{Disability (Personal Level)} \\rightarrow \\text{Handicap (Social Level)}$$\n\n*   *Clinical Example*: A construction worker suffers a spinal cord injury in an accident (Disease). This leads to *paraplegia/loss of lower limb motor function* (Impairment). Because of paraplegia, the worker is *unable to walk or stand* (Disability). Because they cannot walk, they cannot access their workplace and *lose their livelihood and employment* (Handicap).",
