    "id": 24,
    "text": "a)Define EPIDEMIOLOGY &classify epidemiology studies b)Describe COHORT STUDY c) describe a CASE CONTROL STUDY (5+5+5)",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 8,
    "answer": "### 1. DEFINITION OF EPIDEMIOLOGY (JOHN M. LAST)\n\"The study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states or events in specified populations, and the application of this study to the control of health problems.\"\n\n### 2. CLASSIFICATION OF EPIDEMIOLOGICAL STUDIES\n1. **Observational Studies**:\n   * **Descriptive Epidemiology**: Studying the distribution of disease in terms of Time, Place, and Person. Formulates hypothesis.\n   * **Analytical Epidemiology**: Testing hypotheses.\n     * **Case-Control Study**: Retrospective (goes from Effect to Cause). Compares a group of diseased individuals (Cases) with healthy individuals (Controls) regarding past exposure.\n     * **Cohort Study**: Prospective (goes from Cause to Effect). Compares a group of exposed individuals with unexposed individuals over time to see who develops the disease.\n2. **Experimental Studies (Intervention Studies)**:\n   * **Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs)**: Standard experimental model on humans to test drug efficacy or vaccine efficacy.\n   * **Field Trials / Community Trials**: Conducted on healthy individuals in a natural setting (e.g., Salk polio vaccine trial).\n\n### 3. KEY COMPARISONS (CASE-CONTROL VS COHORT)\n* **Direction**: Case-Control is retrospective (Effect -> Cause); Cohort is prospective (Cause -> Effect).\n* **Indicators**: Case-Control yields **Odds Ratio (OR)**; Cohort yields **Relative Risk (RR)**, **Attributable Risk (AR)**, and incidence rates.",
    "page": 4
