    "id": 25,
    "text": "a)Describe the steps involved in conducting a RANDOMIZED CONTROL TRIAL b) Describe various types of clinical trials.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 6,
    "answer": "### RANDOMIZED CONTROLLED TRIAL (RCT)\n\n### DEFINITION\nA Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) is an experimental study design in which eligible participants are **randomly assigned** to either an intervention (experimental) group or a control group, to assess the efficacy or effectiveness of a health intervention (drug, vaccine, procedure, or programme).\n\n### A) STEPS IN CONDUCTING AN RCT\n\n1. **Formulation of Research Question & Hypothesis**: Define the PICO — Population, Intervention, Comparison, and Outcome clearly.\n\n2. **Selection of Study Population**:\n   * Define **Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria**.\n   * Obtain **Ethical Clearance** from the Institutional Ethics Committee (IEC).\n   * Obtain **Informed Consent** from each participant.\n\n3. **Baseline Assessment**: All participants undergo baseline measurement of the primary outcome variable and potential confounders.\n\n4. **Randomization (Most Critical Step)**:\n   * Participants are randomly allocated to intervention or control groups using:\n     * Simple random allocation (lottery, random number tables).\n     * Block/Stratified randomization (ensures balance in important variables like age, sex).\n   * **Purpose**: Eliminates selection bias and ensures comparable groups at baseline.\n\n5. **Blinding (Masking)**:\n   * *Single-blind*: Participants unaware of group assignment.\n   * *Double-blind*: Both participants AND investigators unaware (gold standard for drug trials — prevents observer bias).\n   * *Triple-blind*: Participants, investigators, AND data analysts all blinded.\n   * **Placebo**: Control group receives an identical-looking inactive substance.\n\n6. **Follow-up and Monitoring**:\n   * Both groups followed for a defined period under identical conditions (except for the intervention).\n   * Interim safety analysis by an independent Data Safety Monitoring Board (DSMB).\n\n7. **Outcome Measurement**: Measure the predefined primary and secondary outcomes at the end of the study period.\n\n8. **Statistical Analysis**:\n   * **Intention-to-Treat (ITT) Analysis**: All participants analysed in the group they were randomized to, regardless of compliance — prevents overestimation of efficacy.\n   * **Per-Protocol Analysis**: Only analyser who completed the trial per protocol.\n\n9. **Reporting**: According to **CONSORT (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials)** guidelines — includes CONSORT flow diagram.\n\n### B) TYPES OF CLINICAL TRIALS\n\n| Type | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Phase I** | First in humans; small number of healthy volunteers (or patients with no other options); assesses **safety, pharmacokinetics, dosage**. |\n| **Phase II** | Small patient group (~100–300); assesses **efficacy, dose-response, side effects**. |\n| **Phase III** | Large-scale (**definitive efficacy trial**); randomised, controlled, double-blind; compare new treatment vs. standard/placebo. Required for regulatory approval. |\n| **Phase IV** | **Post-marketing surveillance** after drug is approved; long-term safety, rare adverse effects, and effectiveness in real-world populations. |\n\n### KEY MEASURES IN RCT\n* **Relative Risk (RR)**: Risk in experimental group / Risk in control group.\n* **Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR) = Control rate − Experimental rate**.\n* **Number Needed to Treat (NNT) = 1/ARR** — number of patients needed to treat to prevent one adverse outcome.",
    "page": 4
