    "text": "Add a note MORTALITY RATES",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### MORTALITY RATES\nMortality rates measure the frequency of deaths occurring in a population during a specified time period. Sourced from Park's textbook, they are the most widely used and reliable indicators of health and socio-economic development.\n\n#### Key Mortality Rates and Ratios\n1.  **Crude Death Rate (CDR)**:\n    *   *Formula*: $\\text{CDR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of deaths in a year}}{\\text{Mid-year population}} \\times 1,000$.\n    *   *Limitation*: Highly influenced by the age and sex structure of the population.\n2.  **Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)**:\n    *   *Formula*: $\\text{IMR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of deaths of infants under 1 year during the year}}{\\text{Number of live births during the year}} \\times 1,000$.\n    *   *Uses*: The most sensitive index of a community's health status and sanitary conditions.\n3.  **Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)**:\n    *   *Formula*: $\\text{MMR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of maternal deaths in a year}}{\\text{Number of live births in the same year}} \\times 100,000$.\n    *   *Definition*: Deaths of women during pregnancy, childbirth, or within 42 days of delivery due to pregnancy-related causes.\n4.  **Specific Death Rates**:\n    *   *Age-Specific Death Rate* (e.g., for children aged 1-4 years): $\\frac{\\text{Deaths in age group 1-4 in a year}}{\\text{Mid-year population of age group 1-4}} \\times 1,000$.\n    *   *Cause-Specific Death Rate*: $\\frac{\\text{Deaths due to Tuberculosis in a year}}{\\text{Mid-year population}} \\times 100,000$.\n5.  **Proportional Mortality Rate**:\n    *   *Formula*: $\\frac{\\text{Number of deaths from a specific cause in a year}}{\\text{Total deaths from all causes in the same year}} \\times 100$.\n    *   *Uses*: Represents the relative contribution of a specific disease or age group to the overall mortality of the population.\n6.  **Case Fatality Rate (CFR)**:\n    *   *Formula*: $\\frac{\\text{Number of deaths from a specific disease}}{\\text{Total diagnosed cases of that disease}} \\times 100$. Measures virulence.",
