    "id": 35,
    "text": "Add a note on types of bias in case control and cohort study .",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### TYPES OF BIAS IN CASE-CONTROL AND COHORT STUDIES\nBias is any systematic error in the design, conduct, or analysis of an epidemiological study that results in a mistaken estimate of an exposure's effect on the risk of disease.\n\n#### 1. Common Biases in Case-Control Studies\n1.  **Recall Bias**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: Cases (diseased individuals) are more motivated to remember past events and search for a reason for their illness. Healthy controls often fail to recall past exposures with the same detail.\n2.  **Selection Bias**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: Occurs when the cases or controls chosen are not representative of the source population.\n3.  **Berksonian Bias (Admission Rate Bias)**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: Occurs in hospital-based case-control studies when cases and controls are selected from hospitalized patients who have different rates of exposure and admission.\n4.  **Interviewer Bias**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: The interviewer may query cases more intensely about exposure history if they know the patient's disease status, leading to systematic differences in data collection.\n5.  **Confounding Bias**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: An apparent association is distorted by the presence of a confounding variable.\n\n#### 2. Common Biases in Cohort Studies\n1.  **Attrition Bias (Loss to Follow-up)**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: Loss of subjects over time due to migration, death from other causes, or loss of interest, which may be systematically related to exposure or outcome.\n2.  **Healthy Worker Effect**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: Workers in an occupational cohort are generally healthier than the general population, biasing comparative mortality.\n3.  **Information / Misclassification Bias**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: Changes in diagnostic criteria or error in classifying exposure or outcome over a long follow-up period.\n4.  **Diagnostic Suspicion Bias**:\n    *   *Mechanism*: Knowing a subject is exposed may make the investigator search more intensively for the disease outcome.",
