    "text": "Outline the steps involved in a)Cohort study b)Case control study",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### 1. STEPS IN CONDUCTING A COHORT STUDY (Cause -> Effect)\nA cohort study is a prospective, observational study starting with healthy individuals grouped by exposure to a risk factor.\n1. **Selection of Study Cohorts**: Exposed group (e.g., heavy smokers) and an unexposed comparison cohort (similar demographics but non-smokers).\n2. **Obtaining Baseline Information**: Through interviews, physical exams, and lab tests to confirm all participants are free of the disease at the start.\n3. **Selection of Comparison Group**: Internal (unexposed members of the same cohort) or external (general population).\n4. **Follow-up**: Periodical monitoring of both cohorts using identical methods (questionnaires, check-ups, hospital registers) to identify incident cases.\n5. **Data Analysis**:\n   * *Incidence Rate*: Calculate among exposed ($I_e$) and unexposed ($I_u$).\n   * *Relative Risk (RR)*: $RR = I_e / I_u$. Measures strength of association.\n   * *Attributable Risk (AR)*: $AR = (I_e - I_u) / I_e \\times 100$. Indicates preventable disease load.\n\n### 2. STEPS IN CONDUCTING A CASE-CONTROL STUDY (Effect -> Cause)\nA retrospective, observational study starting with diseased individuals (cases) and comparing them to healthy controls.\n1. **Selection of Cases**: Defined diagnostic criteria, newly diagnosed (incident) cases selected from hospitals or communities.\n2. **Selection of Controls**: Similar to cases but free from the disease under study.\n3. **Matching**: Process of selecting controls to eliminate confounding variables (e.g., matching cases and controls by age, gender, social class).\n4. **Measurement of Exposure**: Interviewing, reviewing history, or laboratory testing to determine past exposure to the risk factor. Done identically for both groups.\n5. **Data Analysis**:\n   * *Exposure rates*: Comparison between groups.\n   * *Odds Ratio (OR)*: Cross-product ratio ($OR = ad / bc$ from a $2 \\times 2$ table). Estimates relative risk when the disease is rare.",
