    "text": "Add a note on types of EPIDEMIC AND EPIDEMIC CURVE",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### 1. EPIDEMIC\n* **Standard Definition**: The occurrence in a community or region of cases of an illness, specific health-related behavior, or other health-related events clearly in excess of normal expectancy.\n* **Etymology**: Derived from Greek (*epi* = upon, *demos* = people).\n* **Example**: Sudden outbreak of Cholera, COVID-19 initial outbreak in Wuhan.\n\n### 2. ENDEMIC\n* **Standard Definition**: The constant presence of a disease or infectious agent within a given geographic area or population group, without external importation.\n* **Etymology**: Derived from Greek (*en* = in, *demos* = people).\n* **Example**: Typhoid in India, Malaria in tropical climates, Endemic Goitre in sub-Himalayan regions.\n\n### 3. PANDEMIC\n* **Standard Definition**: An epidemic occurring over a very wide area, crossing international boundaries, and usually affecting a large number of people on a global scale.\n* **Etymology**: Derived from Greek (*pan* = all, *demos* = people).\n* **Example**: COVID-19 pandemic (2020), Influenza pandemics (H1N1 1918 and 2009).\n\n### 4. SPORADIC\n* **Standard Definition**: The scattered or occasional occurrence of cases of a disease in a population, separated in both space and time, with no clear connection to a common source.\n* **Etymology**: Derived from Greek (*sporadikos* = scattered).\n* **Example**: Tetanus, Rabies, Meningococcal meningitis in non-epidemic years.",
