    "text": "a) Add a note on Cold chain  b)vaccine vial monitors",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 11,
    "answer": "### 1. DEFINITION OF COLD CHAIN\nThe Cold Chain is a system of transporting and storing vaccines at recommended low temperatures ($+2^\\circ\\text{C}$ to $+8^\\circ\\text{C}$ for most vaccines, and $-15^\\circ\\text{C}$ to $-25^\\circ\\text{C}$ for OPV/yellow fever) from the point of manufacture to the point of administration to maintain their potency.\n\n### 2. COLD CHAIN EQUIPMENT\n* **Walk-in Coolers (WIC) and Walk-in Freezers (WIF)**: Regional/district storage.\n* **Ice-Lined Refrigerators (ILR)**: Backbone of the cold chain at PHCs. Holds temperature even during power outages (up to 20+ hours) using ice-lined tubes. **Rule**: Place sensitive vaccines (DPT, TT, Hepatitis B) in the basket, avoiding direct contact with the bottom or sides to prevent freezing.\n* **Deep Freezers**: Used to freeze ice packs and store OPV.\n* **Vaccine Carriers**: Smaller insulated boxes carrying 4 frozen ice packs. Used by health workers for outreach/immunization days.\n\n### 3. VACCINE VIAL MONITOR (VVM)\nA VVM is a chemical indicator label printed directly on the vaccine vial. It changes color gradually upon cumulative heat exposure, warning health workers if a vaccine has exceeded its heat limit.\n* **Stage 1 (Inner square is lighter than outer circle)**: **USE vaccine**.\n* **Stage 2 (Inner square is still lighter but slightly darker)**: **USE vaccine** (use first).\n* **Stage 3 (Inner square matches outer circle)**: **DO NOT USE**.\n* **Stage 4 (Inner square is darker than outer circle)**: **DO NOT USE**.",
