    "text": "Universal Immunization.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology (Principles & Methods)",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### UNIVERSAL IMMUNIZATION PROGRAM (UIP)\nThe Universal Immunization Programme (UIP) is one of the largest public health interventions in the world, launched in India on **November 19, 1985**. It aims to provide free immunization against vaccine-preventable diseases to all infants and pregnant women.\n\n#### 1. Historical Background\n*   Evolved from the Expanded Programme on Immunization (EPI) launched in 1978.\n*   Integrated into the Child Survival and Safe Motherhood (CSSM) program (1992), Reproductive and Child Health (RCH) program (1997), and currently under the **National Health Mission (NHM)**.\n\n#### 2. Core Objectives\n*   To rapidly increase immunization coverage.\n*   To improve the quality of services.\n*   To establish a reliable cold chain system for vaccine storage.\n*   To achieve self-sufficiency in vaccine production.\n*   To eradicate Poliomyelitis and eliminate Neonatal and Maternal Tetanus.\n\n#### 3. Diseases Covered (12 Vaccine-Preventable Diseases)\n*   **Nationally**: Diphtheria, Pertussis, Tetanus, Polio, Tuberculosis, Measles, Hepatitis B, Meningitis and Pneumonia caused by *Haemophilus influenzae* type b (Hib), and Pneumococcal diseases (PCV).\n*   **Sub-nationally/Endemic areas**: Japanese Encephalitis (JE), Rotavirus diarrhea, and Rubella (as MR vaccine).\n\n#### 4. Key Initiatives\n*   **Mission Indradhanush** (launched in 2014) to vaccinate all unvaccinated and partially vaccinated children and pregnant women.\n*   **U-WIN Platform**: A digital platform for tracking every pregnant woman and child's immunization status.",
