    "id": 62,
    "text": "Add a note on LEAD TIME(3/5marks)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Screening for Disease",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### LEAD TIME IN SCREENING\nLead time is the period of time between the early detection of a disease by a screening test and its usual clinical presentation/diagnosis when symptoms occur.\n\n#### 1. Concept of Disease Timeline\n$$\\text{Pathological onset} \\rightarrow \\text{First Screen-detectable point} \\rightarrow [\\text{LEAD TIME}] \\rightarrow \\text{Clinical onset (Symptoms occur)} \\rightarrow \\text{Death/Outcome}$$\n\nThe screening test moves the diagnosis forward in time by the duration of the lead time.\n\n#### 2. Lead Time Bias\n*   **Definition**: A systematic error that arises when evaluating the survival benefits of screening.\n*   *Mechanism*: Screen-detected patients may appear to survive longer (from the date of diagnosis) compared to clinically diagnosed patients, but this apparent survival advantage is simply due to earlier diagnosis (adding the lead time) and not because the intervention delayed death.\n*   *Public Health Implication*: To prove that a screening program is truly effective, one must demonstrate a reduction in **disease-specific mortality**, rather than just an increase in survival time from the date of diagnosis.",
    "page": 7
