    "id": 66,
    "text": "What is Gold Standard Test?(3marks)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Screening for Disease",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### GOLD STANDARD TEST\nA Gold Standard Test is the best available diagnostic test or benchmark procedure that is regarded as standard for determining the presence or absence of a particular disease or condition. It is assumed to be 100% sensitive and 100% specific for the purpose of evaluating new diagnostic tests.\n\n#### 1. Purpose in Diagnostic Studies\n*   Serves as the definitive reference point against which a new, cheaper, faster, or less invasive diagnostic test is compared.\n*   It is used to calculate the screening parameters: **Sensitivity**, **Specificity**, **Positive Predictive Value (PPV)**, and **Negative Predictive Value (NPV)**.\n\n#### 2. Clinical Examples\n*   **Tuberculosis**: Sputum culture for *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* is the gold standard (compared to sputum smear or GeneXpert).\n*   **Coronary Artery Disease**: Coronary angiography is the gold standard (compared to ECG or stress test).\n*   **Breast Cancer**: Histopathological examination of biopsy tissue is the gold standard (compared to mammography).\n*   **Malaria**: Microscopy of Giemsa-stained thick and thin blood smears (compared to rapid diagnostic tests).\n\n#### 3. Limitations\nA gold standard test may be expensive, highly invasive, time-consuming, or require sophisticated laboratory infrastructure. This is why screening tests are developed as alternatives.",
