    "text": "Mention the SOURCES OF HEALTH INFORMATION.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Biostatistics & Health Information",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### Sources of Health Information\nHealth information is essential for planning, monitoring, and evaluating health services. According to **Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine**, the principal sources of health information include:\n\n1. **Census**:\n   * The primary source of demographic and socio-economic data.\n   * Conducted every **10 years** in India (the first synchronous census was in 1881).\n   * It provides comprehensive data on population size, age-sex structure, geographic distribution, literacy, and occupation.\n\n2. **Registration of Vital Events**:\n   * Governed by the **Registration of Births and Deaths (RBD) Act, 1969**, making registration of births, deaths, and stillbirths compulsory.\n   * The statutory timeline for registration is **21 days** from the date of occurrence.\n   * It provides continuous, legal, and permanent records of vital events.\n\n3. **Sample Registration System (SRS)**:\n   * Initiated in India in 1964-65 to overcome the high level of under-registration in civil registration.\n   * Uses a **dual-record system** (continuous recording by a local registrar and an independent half-yearly survey by an investigator).\n   * It provides reliable annual estimates of birth rates, death rates, infant mortality rates, and maternal mortality ratios at national and sub-national levels.\n\n4. **Notification of Diseases**:\n   * Statutory obligation to report specific infectious diseases (e.g., Cholera, Plague, Yellow Fever, Tuberculosis, Malaria) to the local health authority.\n   * Helps in early detection of outbreaks and immediate implementation of control measures.\n\n5. **Hospital Records**:\n   * Provides highly reliable clinical and morbidity data.\n   * **Limitation**: Represents only the \"tip of the iceberg\" since it covers only patients who seek hospital care, omitting subclinical cases or those using alternative systems.\n\n6. **Disease Registries**:\n   * Organized systems that collect and maintain clinical data on patients with specific conditions.\n   * *Examples*: **National Cancer Registry Programme (NCRP)**, Tuberculosis registers, stroke registries, and blindness registries.\n\n7. **Health Surveys**:\n   * Ad-hoc or periodic surveys designed to collect specific health, demographic, and nutritional information.\n   * *Examples*: **National Family Health Surveys (NFHS)**, District Level Household Surveys (DLHS), and Longitudinal Ageing Study in India (LASI).\n\n8. **Surveillance Systems**:\n   * Ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, and interpretation of health data.\n   * *Example*: **Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP)** for communicable diseases.\n\n9. **Other Sources**:\n   * **School Health Records**: Information on growth, nutrition, and morbidity of school children.\n   * **Industrial Health Records**: Morbidity, occupational hazards, and absenteeism data.\n   * **Environmental Monitoring Systems**: Data on air/water pollution, radiation levels, and meteorological parameters.",
