    "text": "Add a note on types of data presentation.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Biostatistics & Health Information",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Types of Data Presentation\nData presentation in biostatistics is the process of organizing and displaying collected data in a clear, concise, and professional format. According to biostatistics principles, the three primary types of data presentation are:\n\n1. **Tabulation (Tables)**:\n   * **Simple Table**: Displays a frequency distribution for a single variable (e.g., number of malaria cases classified by age group).\n   * **Complex Table (Cross-tabulation)**: Displays the relationship between two or more variables simultaneously (e.g., number of malaria cases classified by age group, gender, and treatment outcome).\n   * *Essential Components*: Every standard table must include a **Title** (clear and descriptive), **Stubs** (row headings), **Captions** (column headings), the **Body** (numerical cells), **Footnotes** (to explain symbols/abbreviations), and the **Source of Data**.\n\n2. **Diagrams (Qualitative or Discrete Quantitative Data)**:\n   * **Bar Chart / Diagram**: Rectangular bars are drawn horizontally or vertically. The width is uniform, and the height/length is proportional to the values. **Spaces are left between bars** because the variable is discrete.\n     * *Simple Bar Chart*: For single comparisons.\n     * *Multiple Bar Chart*: For grouped comparisons side-by-side.\n     * *Component / Stacked Bar Chart*: Where bars are partitioned to show the composition of the total.\n   * **Pie Chart**: A circular chart divided into sectors. The angle of each sector at the center is proportional to the percentage share ($\text{Angle} = \text{Percentage} \\times 3.6^\\circ$). Best for representing \"parts of a whole\" (e.g., causes of infant mortality).\n   * **Pictogram**: Uses small, simplified icons or pictures to represent values (e.g., one child icon = 1,000 children immunized).\n   * **Map Diagram (Cartogram)**: Displays geographic or spatial distribution of a variable (e.g., endemicity of filariasis in a state map).\n\n3. **Graphs (Continuous Quantitative Data)**:\n   * **Histogram**: Rectangular bars representing the frequency distribution of a continuous variable (e.g., blood pressure, hemoglobin levels). The bars are drawn adjacent to each other with **no spaces in between**, representing the continuous scale. The area of the bar is proportional to the frequency.\n   * **Frequency Polygon**: Obtained by connecting the midpoints of the tops of the histogram rectangles with straight lines. It smooths out the distribution and is useful for comparing two or more datasets.\n   * **Line Diagram**: Shows trends over a continuous variable, usually time (e.g., Infant Mortality Rate trends in India over the last three decades).\n   * **Scatter Diagram (Correlation Graph)**: Plots individual coordinate points of two continuous variables to analyze their relationship (e.g., relationship between blood pressure and salt intake).",
