    "text": "Add a note on NORMAL DISTRIBUTION CURVE.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Biostatistics & Health Information",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### THE NORMAL DISTRIBUTION CURVE (GAUSSIAN CURVE)\nA fundamental statistical concept in biostatistics used to describe the distribution of continuous biological variables (e.g., blood pressure, height, hemoglobin levels).\n* **Key Characteristics**:\n  1. **Symmetry**: The curve is perfectly symmetrical, bell-shaped, and asymptotic (ends never touch the horizontal axis).\n  2. **Coincidence of Central Values**: The Mean, Median, and Mode are all equal and lie exactly at the center of the curve.\n  3. **Standard Deviation Ranges**:\n     * $\\text{Mean} \\pm 1\\text{ SD}$ covers **68.27%** of all observations in the population.\n     * $\\text{Mean} \\pm 2\\text{ SD}$ covers **95.45%** of all observations in the population.\n     * $\\text{Mean} \\pm 3\\text{ SD}$ covers **99.73%** of all observations in the population.\n  4. **Total Area**: The total area under the normal curve is equal to 1 (or 100%).",
