    "text": "Write about measures of CENTRAL TENDENCY .(Mean ,median ,mode)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Biostatistics & Health Information",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### Measures of Central Tendency\nMeasures of central tendency are statistical parameters that identify the single value representing the center, middle, or most typical value of a frequency distribution. The three primary measures are **Mean, Median, and Mode**.\n\n#### 1. Arithmetic Mean\n*   **Definition**: The sum of all observations in a dataset divided by the total number of observations.\n*   **Formula**:\n    $$\\bar{X} = \\frac{\\sum X}{N}$$\n    *(where $\\sum X$ is the sum of observations and $N$ is the total sample size)*\n*   **Characteristics**:\n    *   It is mathematically stable, easy to calculate, and uses every single value in the dataset.\n    *   **Major Limitation**: Highly sensitive to extreme values (outliers). A few extremely high or low values will pull the mean, making it unrepresentative of skewed data.\n\n#### 2. Median\n*   **Definition**: The middle-most value of the dataset when all observations are arranged in ascending or descending order.\n*   **Calculation**:\n    *   If the number of observations ($N$) is **odd**, the median is the $\\frac{N+1}{2}^{th}$ value.\n    *   If the number of observations ($N$) is **even**, the median is the average of the two middle values, i.e., the $\\frac{N}{2}^{th}$ and $\\frac{N}{2} + 1^{th}$ values.\n*   **Characteristics**:\n    *   It divides the distribution into two equal halves (50% of values are below the median, and 50% are above).\n    *   **Major Advantage**: It is **not affected by extreme values (outliers)**. Therefore, it is the best measure of central tendency for highly skewed distributions (e.g., incubation period of acute infectious diseases, family income, hospital stay duration).\n\n#### 3. Mode\n*   **Definition**: The value that occurs with the highest frequency in a dataset.\n*   **Characteristics**:\n    *   A dataset can have no mode, one mode (unimodal), or multiple modes (bimodal or multimodal).\n    *   Mainly used for **qualitative/categorical data** to describe the most popular option or category (e.g., the most common presenting symptom in cholera cases).\n    *   It is unstable and not widely used in advanced mathematical statistics.\n\n#### Relationship in Skewed Distributions\n*   In a **perfectly symmetrical (Normal) distribution**:\n    $$\\text{Mean} = \\text{Median} = \\text{Mode}$$\n*   In a **moderately skewed distribution**:\n    $$\\text{Mode} = 3 \\times \\text{Median} - 2 \\times \\text{Mean}$$\n*   In a **positively skewed distribution** (skewed to the right):\n    $$\\text{Mean} > \\text{Median} > \\text{Mode}$$\n*   In a **negatively skewed distribution** (skewed to the left):\n    $$\\text{Mode} > \\text{Median} > \\text{Mean}$$",
    "page": 8
