    "text": "Describe RAPID SAND FILTRATION",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Environment and Health",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### RAPID SAND FILTRATION (MECHANICAL/PATTERSON FILTER)\nA high-rate mechanical water purification system suited for turbid waters.\n* **Steps in Operation**:\n  1. **Coagulation**: Alum (aluminum sulfate) is added ($5-40\\text{ mg/l}$) to form sticky gelatinous precipitates (flocs).\n  2. **Rapid Mixing / Flocculation**: Water is agitated to allow flocs to trap suspended particles and bacteria.\n  3. **Sedimentation**: Water settles for 2-4 hours; flocs precipitate to the bottom.\n  4. **Filtration**: Water passes through a rapid sand filter at rates 40 times faster than slow sand filters.\n  5. **Backwashing (Cleaning)**: Done when the filter is clogged (\"loss of head\" exceeds limit). High-velocity clean water and compressed air are pumped upward from the bottom of the filter bed to wash out trapped mud and flocs.",
