    "text": "Rapid sand filtration vs slow sand filtration.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Environment and Health",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### RAPID SAND FILTRATION vs SLOW SAND FILTRATION\n\n| Parameter | Slow Sand Filtration (SSF) | Rapid Sand Filtration (RSF) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Also Known As** | Biological/English Filter | Mechanical/American Filter |\n| **Filtration Rate** | 0.1–0.4 m³/m²/hour (very slow) | 5–15 m³/m²/hour (50× faster) |\n| **Area Required** | Very large area | Smaller area |\n| **Mechanism** | Biological — **Schmutzdecke** (biological mat of algae, bacteria, protozoa) at the surface does the purification | Mechanical straining by the sand; requires coagulation + flocculation before filtration |\n| **Pre-treatment** | Sedimentation only (no coagulation) | Coagulation + Flocculation + Sedimentation are MANDATORY before RSF |\n| **Turbidity** | Can only handle low turbidity water (<10 NTU) | Can handle highly turbid water (up to 30–40 NTU) |\n| **Bacteria Removal** | ~99% (excellent) | ~90–95% (good, but less than SSF) |\n| **Sand Depth** | 0.6–1.2 m (deep) | 0.6–1.8 m |\n| **Support Gravel** | 0.3–0.45 m | 0.45–0.6 m |\n| **Cleaning** | Scrape top 2–5 cm of Schmutzdecke; washed, dried, replaced. (Every 4–8 weeks) | **Back-washing** with filtered water (Every 24–48 hours — frequent) |\n| **Downtime for Cleaning** | Longer downtime (Schmutzdecke needs 2–4 weeks to re-establish) | Minimal downtime; back-washing takes minutes |\n| **Cost** | Low running cost; High capital cost (large area) | High running cost (pumps, coagulants); smaller area |\n| **Maintenance** | Simple; less skilled labour needed | Requires skilled operators and pumping equipment |\n| **Suitable For** | Rural/small urban water supplies | Large urban municipal water treatment |\n\n### KEY POINT FOR EXAMS\n* The **Schmutzdecke** (\"dirty skin\" in German) is unique to **Slow Sand Filtration** — it is the critical biological film that ensures excellent bacterial removal. It is NOT present in RSF.\n* Chlorination is done AFTER both types of filtration for final disinfection.",
