    "text": "Describe Chlorination of water by a)SUPER CHLORINATION  b)BREAK POINT CHLORINATION c)RESIDUAL CHLORINE",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Environment and Health",
    "stars": 10,
    "answer": "### 1. CHLORINATION OF WATER\nChlorination is the most widely used chemical method of disinfecting drinking water. It kills pathogenic organisms, oxidizes iron/manganese, and prevents algae growth.\n* **Mechanism**: Chlorine gas or hypochlorite is added to water. It reacts with water to form **Hypochlorous Acid (HOCl)**, which is the active germicidal agent.\n* **Break-point Chlorination**: The process of adding chlorine until the chlorine demand of the water is fully met. \n  * *Breakpoint*: The point where all ammonia, organic compounds, and reducing agents are fully oxidized, and any further addition of chlorine results in direct **Free Residual Chlorine** ($0.5\\text{ mg/l}$ after a **contact time of 30 minutes**).\n* **Super-chlorination**: Adding high concentrations of chlorine during epidemics/floods; requires subsequent de-chlorination before drinking.",
