    "id": 127,
    "text": "Disposal Of Waste From Injection Room.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Biomedical Waste & Disaster Management",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Disposal of Waste from Injection Room\n\nInjection rooms in healthcare facilities generate a diverse mix of biomedical waste. Under the **Biomedical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (amended)**, segregation at the point of generation is statutory. The disposal protocol is categorized by color-coded streams:\n\n*   **Red Non-Chlorinated Plastic Bag (Recyclable Plastics):**\n    *   *Items:* Plastic syringes (without needles/cut sharps), IV administration sets, saline bottles, plastic vials, and catheters.\n    *   *Treatment & Disposal:* Autoclaving, microwaving, or hydroclaving to achieve disinfection, followed by shredding or mutilation. The shredded plastic is sent to registered recyclers for manufacturing non-clinical items.\n*   **Yellow Non-Chlorinated Plastic Bag (Soiled Waste):**\n    *   *Items:* Cotton swabs, gauze, dressings contaminated with blood or body fluids, and disposable face masks/gloves.\n    *   *Treatment & Disposal:* High-temperature incineration at a Common Biomedical Waste Treatment Facility (CBWTF) or deep burial in designated remote rural areas.\n*   **White Translucent Puncture-Proof Container (Waste Sharps):**\n    *   *Items:* Needles, syringes with fixed/attached needles, scalpels, blades, lancets, and guide wires.\n    *   *Treatment & Disposal:* Autoclaving or dry-heat sterilization, followed by shredding, mutilation, or chemical encapsulation. The sterile residue is sent to iron foundries or designated sharp-disposal pits.\n*   **Blue Cardboard Box/Puncture-Proof Container (Glass Waste):**\n    *   *Items:* Broken ampoules, glass vials, slide covers, and glass syringes.\n    *   *Treatment & Disposal:* Disinfection by autoclaving or soaking in 1% sodium hypochlorite solution, followed by washing and sending to glass recyclers.\n*   **Black Bin / Municipal Waste Container:**\n    *   *Items:* Non-infectious waste such as paper wrappers, cardboard boxes, plastic wrappers of syringes, and clean packaging.\n    *   *Treatment & Disposal:* Placed in the municipal solid waste stream for landfill disposal.\n\n**Safety Precautions:** Needles must never be recapped manually. They should be cut or bent immediately at the point of use using a mechanical needle cutter to prevent accidental needle-stick injuries.",
    "page": 13
