    "text": "Enlist community nutrition programs in India",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Nutrition and Health",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### Community Nutrition Programs in India\n\nIndia has implemented several community nutrition programs to combat protein-energy malnutrition (PEM), micronutrient deficiencies, and food insecurity. These programs are categorized by their primary objectives:\n\n#### 1. Supplementary Nutrition Programs\n*   **Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS):** Launched in 1975, this is the largest program of its kind. It delivers supplementary nutrition through Anganwadi centers in the form of Take-Home Rations (THR) or Hot Cooked Meals (HCM) to children (6 months to 6 years) and pregnant/lactating mothers.\n*   **PM POSHAN (formerly the Mid-Day Meal Scheme):** Provides hot, cooked meals to school children in classes I to VIII in government and government-aided schools, aiming to improve nutritional status and boost school enrollment.\n*   **Special Nutrition Programme (SNP):** Targets preschool children and pregnant/lactating mothers in urban slums and tribal areas.\n*   **Balwadi Nutrition Programme:** Administered through voluntary organizations for children aged 3–5 years.\n\n#### 2. Micronutrient Prophylaxis Programs\n*   **National Prophylaxis Programme Against Nutritional Blindness due to Vitamin A Deficiency (Vitamin A Prophylaxis Program):** Launched in 1970, this program administers massive periodic oral doses of Vitamin A to children aged 9–59 months.\n*   **National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP):** Focuses on the universal iodization of common salt to prevent goiter, cretinism, and other iodine deficiency disorders.\n*   **Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB):** Under the National Nutritional Mission (POSHAN Abhiyaan), this program focuses on reducing anemia across life stages using a **6x6x6 strategy** (IFA supplementation, deworming, and behavior change communication).\n\n#### 3. Food Security Programs\n*   **Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS):** Provides subsidized food grains (wheat, rice, sugar) to low-income families (Below Poverty Line - BPL and Antyodaya Anna Yojana - AAY cardholders).\n*   **National Food Security Act (NFSA), 2013:** Legalizes the right to receive subsidized food grains for up to 75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population.",
    "page": 14
