    "id": 137,
    "text": "Describe vitamin A deficiency disorder (XEROPTHALMIA), its prevention and control",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Nutrition and Health",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### Vitamin A Deficiency (Xerophthalmia): Clinical Features, Prevention, and Control\n\nVitamin A deficiency (VAD) is a major public health problem in developing countries and the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness. \n\n#### 1. WHO Classification of Xerophthalmia\nXerophthalmia encompasses all the ocular manifestations of Vitamin A deficiency:\n\n*   **XN:** **Night Blindness (Nyctalopia):** The earliest functional symptom. It is caused by impaired regeneration of rhodopsin in the rods.\n*   **X1A:** **Conjunctival Xerosis:** Dryness, dullness, and loss of luster of the bulbar conjunctiva.\n*   **X1B:** **Bitot's Spots:** Small, dry, foamy, triangular, pearly-white plaques on the bulbar conjunctiva, usually temporal to the cornea.\n*   **X2:** **Corneal Xerosis:** Dry, hazy, and dull appearance of the cornea.\n*   **X3A:** **Corneal Ulceration / Keratomalacia (affecting $< 1/3$ of the corneal surface):** Softening and melting of the cornea, leading to ulceration.\n*   **X3B:** **Corneal Ulceration / Keratomalacia (affecting $\\ge 1/3$ of the corneal surface):** Extensive necrosis of the cornea; can lead to perforation and loss of the eye.\n*   **XS:** **Corneal Scarring:** Healed scars from past corneal lesions, causing permanent blindness.\n*   **XF:** **Xerophthalmic Fundus:** Small, pale yellow lesions scattered across the retina (late stage).\n\n#### 2. Prevention of Vitamin A Deficiency\n\n##### A. National Prophylaxis Programme against Nutritional Blindness\nThis program administers periodic, high-dose oral Vitamin A supplements to children aged 9 months to 5 years (a total of 9 doses):\n*   **1st Dose (at 9 months with Measles/Rubella vaccine):** **1,00,000 IU** (1 ml) orally.\n*   **2nd Dose (at 18 months with DPT booster):** **2,00,000 IU** (2 ml) orally.\n*   **3rd to 9th Doses (every 6 months up to 5 years of age):** **2,00,000 IU** (2 ml) orally per dose.\n\n##### B. Dietary Diversification and Fortification\n*   Encourage the consumption of Vitamin A-rich foods: animal sources (milk, egg yolk, liver, butter) and beta-carotene-rich plant sources (carrots, sweet potatoes, green leafy vegetables, ripe papaya, mango).\n*   Fortification of staple foods, such as edible oils and milk, with Vitamin A (as mandated by FSSAI).\n\n#### 3. Treatment of Active Xerophthalmia\nFor any child diagnosed with active xerophthalmia (except XN or X1B without active lesions), high-dose Vitamin A must be administered immediately according to the WHO treatment schedule:\n*   **Day 1 (immediately on diagnosis):** **2,00,000 IU** orally (1,00,000 IU for infants $< 12$ months).\n*   **Day 2:** Repeat the same dose (**2,00,000 IU** orally).\n*   **Day 14 to 28:** Administer a third dose (**2,00,000 IU** orally) to restore liver stores.",
