    "text": "Add a note on food adulteration .prevention& control of food adulteration diseases",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Nutrition and Health",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### Food Adulteration: Common Diseases, Prevention, and Control\n\n**Food Adulteration** is the deliberate addition of inferior, cheaper, or toxic substances to food, or the removal of a valuable constituent from it, which compromises its quality, safety, and nutritional value.\n\n#### 1. Major Adulterants and Associated Diseases\n\n##### A. Argemone Oil (in Mustard Oil) -> Endemic Dropsy\n*   *Toxin:* **Sanguinarine**. This alkaloid inhibits Na+/K+-ATPase, increases capillary permeability, and causes capillary dilatation.\n*   *Clinical Features:* Sudden onset of bilateral pitting edema of the lower limbs, erythema, localized skin warmth, diarrhea, tachycardia, cardiac failure, and **glaucoma** (due to increased intraocular pressure).\n\n##### B. Lathyrus sativus (Khesari Dal in Bengal Gram) -> Neurolathyrism\n*   *Toxin:* **BOAA** (beta-N-oxalyl-amino-L-alanine). An excitotoxin that causes irreversible motor neuron degeneration in the lumbar spinal cord.\n*   *Clinical Features:* Spastic paraplegia of the lower limbs, progressing from calf stiffness to walking with one or two sticks, and finally to a crawling stage.\n\n##### C. Metanil Yellow (Synthetic non-permitted coal tar dye in turmeric/pulses)\n*   *Toxicity:* Carcinogenic, neurotoxic, and causes testicular degeneration.\n\n##### D. Chicory (in Coffee)\n*   *Toxicity:* Can cause gastrointestinal irritation and joint pains.\n\n#### 2. Prevention and Control of Food Adulteration\n\n##### A. Legislative Framework: FSSAI\n*   The **Food Safety and Standards Act (FSSA), 2006** consolidated older, fragmented laws (such as the *Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954*) and established the **Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI)**.\n*   FSSAI sets science-based standards for food, licenses food businesses, and monitors compliance.\n\n##### B. Surveillance and Enforcement\n*   **Food Safety Officers (FSOs)** collect random food samples from markets and send them to state food testing laboratories.\n*   Severe penalties, fines, and imprisonment are imposed on violators under the FSSA, 2006.\n\n##### C. Quality Marks and Consumer Awareness\n*   Consumers are encouraged to look for quality assurance seals:\n    *   **AGMARK:** For raw agricultural products (ghee, spices, mustard oil).\n    *   **ISI Mark (BIS):** For processed and packaged food products (packaged water, milk powder).\n*   Promoting simple home detection tests (e.g., adding concentrated nitric acid to mustard oil; a reddish-brown color indicates Argemone oil contamination).",
    "page": 15
