    "text": "Nutritional status assessment            (MNEMONIC:cabfave.)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Nutrition and Health",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### Nutritional Status Assessment\n\nThe nutritional status of a community or individual is assessed using a combination of direct and indirect methods. These methods can be systematically remembered using the mnemonic **CABFAVE**:\n\n*   **C - Clinical Assessment:**\n    *   Physical examination to detect signs of nutritional deficiencies.\n    *   *Examples:* Bitot's spots (Vitamin A), pale conjunctiva (Iron/Anemia), thyroid enlargement (Iodine/Goiter), angular stomatitis (Riboflavin), rickets (Vitamin D), and severe muscle wasting (Protein-Energy Malnutrition).\n*   **A - Anthropometric Measurements:**\n    *   Physical measurements of the human body.\n    *   *Examples:* Body weight, height (or recumbent length), Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC), head and chest circumference, skinfold thickness, and indices like Body Mass Index (BMI), Weight-for-Age, and Height-for-Age.\n*   **B - Biochemical Assessment:**\n    *   Laboratory tests of body fluids to detect subclinical deficiencies before clinical signs appear.\n    *   *Examples:* Hemoglobin levels (for anemia), serum albumin (for protein status), serum retinol (for Vitamin A), and urinary iodine excretion (for iodine status).\n*   **F - Functional Indicators:**\n    *   Assessing physiological functions that depend on nutritional adequacy.\n    *   *Examples:* Dark adaptation time (Vitamin A), cognitive tests (Iron/Iodine), physical work capacity, and immunologic response tests.\n*   **A - Assessment of Dietary Intake (Dietary Survey):**\n    *   Quantifying actual food and nutrient intake over a specific period.\n    *   *Methods:* 24-hour dietary recall, weighment of raw/cooked foods, and food frequency questionnaires.\n*   **V - Vital Statistics (Indirect Indicators):**\n    *   Analyzing health and mortality indices that reflect community nutrition.\n    *   *Examples:* Infant Mortality Rate (IMR), Under-5 Mortality Rate, Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR), and cause-specific mortality rates related to malnutrition.\n*   **E - Ecological Factors:**\n    *   Investigating environmental, socioeconomic, and cultural factors that influence nutritional status.\n    *   *Examples:* Family income, size of the household, local food production patterns, cultural food taboos, sanitation, and access to clean water.",
