    "text": "Reference Man and Women.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Nutrition and Health",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Reference Indian Man and Reference Indian Woman\n\nThe Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR) and the National Institute of Nutrition (NIN) define the \"Reference Indian Man\" and \"Reference Indian Woman\" to calculate daily energy, protein, and micronutrient requirements (RDA) for the Indian population. These standards were updated in the **ICMR-NIN 2020 Guidelines**.\n\n#### 1. Reference Indian Man (ICMR-NIN 2020)\nA reference Indian man is defined by the following characteristics:\n*   **Age:** 19 to 39 years.\n*   **Body Weight:** **65 kg** (increased from the previous standard of 60 kg).\n*   **Height:** **1.77 meters** (177 cm).\n*   **Body Mass Index (BMI):** **$18.5 - 22.9\\text{ kg/m}^2$** (normal range).\n*   **Activity Level:** Performs moderate or sedentary physical activity daily.\n*   **Health Status:** Free from active disease, clinically healthy, and physically fit for active work.\n\n#### 2. Reference Indian Woman (ICMR-NIN 2020)\nA reference Indian woman is defined by the following characteristics:\n*   **Age:** 19 to 39 years.\n*   **Body Weight:** **55 kg** (increased from the previous standard of 50 kg).\n*   **Height:** **1.62 meters** (162 cm).\n*   **Body Mass Index (BMI):** **$18.5 - 22.9\\text{ kg/m}^2$** (normal range).\n*   **Activity Level:** Performs moderate or sedentary physical activity daily.\n*   **Physiological State:** Non-pregnant and non-lactating.\n*   **Health Status:** Free from active disease, clinically healthy, and physically fit for active work.\n\n#### 3. Nutritional Significance\nThese baseline parameters serve as the foundation for establishing recommended nutrient intakes. For example, the daily protein recommendation is calculated at **0.83 g/kg body weight**, translating to **54 g/day** for the reference man and **46 g/day** for the reference woman.",
    "page": 15
