    "id": 161,
    "text": "Enumerate genetic diseases .what are the preventive measures? (5Marks)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Genetics, Mental Health & Social Medicine",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Classification of Genetic Diseases and Preventive Measures\n\nGenetic diseases are caused by abnormalities in an individual's genetic material (DNA or chromosomes). They contribute significantly to infant mortality and long-term childhood disability.\n\n#### 1. Enumeration and Classification of Genetic Diseases\n\n##### A. Chromosomal Abnormalities (Numerical or Structural)\n*   *Autosomal Aneuploidies:* Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21), Edwards Syndrome (Trisomy 18), and Patau Syndrome (Trisomy 13).\n*   *Sex Chromosomal Aneuploidies:* Klinefelter Syndrome ($47, XXY$) and Turner Syndrome ($45, X0$).\n\n##### B. Single-Gene (Mendelian) Disorders (Monogenic)\n*   *Autosomal Dominant:* Huntington's Disease, Marfan Syndrome, Achondroplasia, and Neurofibromatosis.\n*   *Autosomal Recessive:* Thalassemia major, Sickle Cell Anemia, Cystic Fibrosis, and Phenylketonuria (PKU).\n*   *X-Linked Recessive:* Hemophilia A and B, Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD), and Red-Green Color Blindness.\n\n##### C. Multifactorial / Polygenic Disorders\nThese result from the interaction of multiple genes with environmental factors:\n*   Congenital anomalies (Cleft lip/palate, Neural Tube Defects).\n*   Adult onset chronic diseases (Essential hypertension, Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, Coronary Artery Disease).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Preventive and Control Measures\n\n##### A. Primary Prevention (Pre-conception / Population Level)\n*   **Avoidance of Consanguineous Marriages:** Minimizes the risk of autosomal recessive disorders by reducing the chance that two carriers of the same rare mutation will reproduce.\n*   **Pre-conceptional Folic Acid Supplementation:** Daily intake of **400 $\\mu$g of folic acid** starting at least 1 month before conception and through the first trimester reduces the risk of Neural Tube Defects (NTDs) by 70%.\n*   **Protection against Teratogens:** Educating pregnant women to avoid exposure to ionizing radiation, teratogenic drugs (e.g., thalidomide, isotretinoin), alcohol, tobacco, and infections (e.g., Rubella, Toxoplasmosis).\n*   **Prospective Genetic Counselling:** Identifying carrier couples through pre-marital screening.\n\n##### B. Secondary Prevention (Prenatal & Neonatal Screening)\n*   **Prenatal Screening & Diagnosis:**\n    *   *First/Second Trimester Screening:* Double/Triple marker tests and ultrasound (Nuchal Translucency scan).\n    *   *Invasive Testing:* Amniocentesis or Chorionic Villus Sampling (CVS) followed by karyotyping or genetic analysis when screening is positive.\n    *   *Non-Invasive Prenatal Testing (NIPT):* Analyzing cell-free fetal DNA in maternal blood.\n    *   *Action:* Offering selective termination under the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) Act if a severe, incurable genetic anomaly is detected.\n*   **Universal Newborn Screening:** Testing heel-prick blood spots shortly after birth to identify treatable metabolic disorders (e.g., Congenital Hypothyroidism, Phenylketonuria, Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia) before irreversible damage occurs.\n\n##### C. Tertiary Prevention (Management of Complications)\n*   Early medical interventions to improve quality of life: dietary restriction of phenylalanine in PKU, thyroid hormone replacement in congenital hypothyroidism, and regular blood transfusions combined with iron chelation therapy in Thalassemia major.",
    "page": 16
