    "text": "DRUG ADDICTION .(3marks)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Genetics, Mental Health & Social Medicine",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Technical Note on Drug Addiction\n\n**Drug Addiction** is defined by the **World Health Organization (WHO)** as:\n> \"A state of periodic or chronic intoxication produced by the repeated consumption of a drug (natural or synthetic), characterized by an overpowering desire or need (compulsion) to continue taking the drug and to obtain it by any means.\"\n\n#### Core Characteristics of Drug Addiction:\n1.  **Compulsion / Craving:** An overwhelming, obsessive drive to procure and use the substance, overriding all other personal, social, and professional responsibilities.\n2.  **Tolerance:** A physiological adaptation where the body requires progressively larger doses of the drug to achieve the same physical or psychological effect.\n3.  **Dependence:**\n    *   *Psychological Dependence:* A mental state where the individual feels they need the drug to function, cope with stress, or achieve pleasure.\n    *   *Physical Dependence:* An altered physiological state where body tissues require the continuous presence of the drug to function normally.\n4.  **Withdrawal Syndrome:** A combination of painful physical and psychological symptoms that occur when the drug is abruptly discontinued (e.g., tremors, sweating, hallucinations, convulsions).\n5.  **Social and Personal Harm:** Causes severe, detrimental consequences to the addict's health, family relationships, financial stability, and society.\n\n*Commonly Abused Substances:* Opioids (heroin, morphine), Cannabinoids (marijuana, hashish), CNS Stimulants (cocaine, amphetamines), and Sedative-Hypnotics (barbiturates, benzodiazepines).",
    "page": 16
