    "chapter": "Genetics, Mental Health & Social Medicine",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Mental Health as a Community Problem\n\nMental health disorders represent a major, under-recognized public health challenge in India and globally, contributing to a high burden of disability, economic loss, and social exclusion.\n\n#### 1. Magnitude of the Problem (Epidemiology)\n*   **National Mental Health Survey (NMHS) of India (2015-16):**\n    *   The lifetime prevalence of mental disorders in India is **$13.7\\%$**.\n    *   Nearly **$10.6\\%$** of the adult population currently has an active mental disorder.\n    *   **The Treatment Gap:** An exceptionally high percentage of individuals with mental illness (**$70\\%$ to $90\\%$** depending on the condition) do not receive appropriate care. This is due to a shortage of professionals, lack of facilities, and social stigma.\n*   **Disability-Adjusted Life Years (DALYs):** Mental disorders contribute to a large share of the global disease burden measured in Years Lived with Disability (YLDs), particularly in the 15–49 age group.\n\n#### 2. Major Categories of Mental Disorders in the Community\n*   **Common Mental Disorders (CMDs):** Depression, generalized anxiety disorder (GAD), panic disorder, phobias, and somatoform disorders. These are highly prevalent at the primary care level.\n*   **Severe Mental Disorders (SMDs):** Schizophrenia, Bipolar Affective Disorder (BPAD), and severe psychoses.\n*   **Substance Use Disorders:** Dependence on alcohol, tobacco, and illicit drugs (cannabis, opioids).\n*   **Child and Adolescent Disorders:** ADHD, autism spectrum disorders, learning disabilities, and conduct disorders.\n*   **Geriatric Disorders:** Dementia, Alzheimer's disease, and late-life depression.\n\n#### 3. Determinants / Contributing Factors\n*   **Socio-Economic Factors:** Poverty, unemployment, lack of education, rapid urbanization, domestic violence, migration, and the breakdown of traditional joint family support networks.\n*   **Biological Factors:** Genetic predisposition, neurochemical imbalances, and chronic physical illnesses.\n*   **Psychological Factors:** Severe emotional trauma, early childhood abuse, chronic stress, and poor coping mechanisms.\n\n#### 4. Barriers to Care and Strategic Solutions\n*   **Major Barriers:** A severe shortage of specialists (only ~0.75 psychiatrists per 100,000 population in India, compared to the recommended standard of $\\ge 3.0$), lack of primary-care integration, and widespread social stigma.\n*   **Strategic Solutions:**\n    *   Integrating mental healthcare with primary health services through the **District Mental Health Programme (DMHP)**.\n    *   Digital health initiatives, such as the **Tele-MANAS** 24/7 helpline, to provide remote counseling.\n    *   Training community health workers (ASHAs and ANMs) for early screening and public anti-stigma campaigns.",
