    "text": "Describe TUBERCULOSIS under the following headings: a)Epidemiology And Epidemiological Indices . B) Tuberculin And Mantoux Test C)Screening And Diagnostic Modalities D) Prevention And Control.(Anti Tubercular Therapy) { Study Inconjugation With RNTCP/NTEP}",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 10,
    "answer": "### TUBERCULOSIS (TB) — COMPREHENSIVE STUDY\n*Based on Park's SPM and NTEP/RNTCP Guidelines*\n\n---\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY & EPIDEMIOLOGICAL INDICES\n\n* **Agent**: *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* (acid-fast bacillus, MTB). Humans are the primary reservoir.\n* **Host Factors**: Malnutrition, HIV co-infection (highest risk), diabetes, steroid use, poverty, and tobacco smoking increase susceptibility.\n* **Environmental Factors**: Overcrowding, poor ventilation, damp dark housing, high population density.\n* **Transmission**: Droplet nuclei (1–5 microns) expelled by smear-positive pulmonary TB patients during coughing, sneezing, or singing.\n\n**Epidemiological Indices:**\n* **Annual Risk of Tuberculosis Infection (ARTI)**: The probability of a person being newly infected with TB during a year. In India, ARTI is approximately 1–1.5%.\n* **Annual Parasite Incidence (not for TB)**: For TB, the key index is **Case Detection Rate (CDR)** and **Treatment Success Rate**.\n* **Notification Rate**: Number of newly notified TB cases per 100,000 population per year.\n\n---\n\n### B) TUBERCULIN & MANTOUX TEST\n\n* **Principle**: Delayed-type hypersensitivity (Type IV) reaction to tuberculo-protein antigen.\n* **Method**: Intradermal injection of **0.1 ml of Purified Protein Derivative (PPD)** (5 TU of RT23) on the volar (inner) aspect of the left forearm.\n* **Reading**: Read at **48–72 hours** by measuring the **transverse diameter of the indurated area** (not erythema).\n* **Interpretation**:\n  * *< 10 mm*: Negative (no significant infection in non-immunosuppressed persons).\n  * *10–14 mm*: Doubtful/equivocal — repeat or consider clinical context.\n  * *≥ 15 mm (or ≥ 10 mm in children)*: Positive — indicates past or present infection with MTB.\n  * *In HIV patients*: ≥ 5 mm is considered positive.\n* **False Positives**: BCG vaccination, non-tuberculous mycobacteria.\n* **False Negatives**: HIV/AIDS, malnutrition, severe TB (anergy), steroid use.\n\n---\n\n### C) SCREENING & DIAGNOSTIC MODALITIES\n\n| Method | When Used | Details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sputum Smear Microscopy (ZN Stain)** | Symptomatic persons with cough > 2 weeks | Detects acid-fast bacilli (AFB). Cheap and rapid. Low sensitivity (~50–80%). |\n| **Sputum Culture (MGIT/LJ Medium)** | Gold Standard for confirmation | Most accurate; identifies drug resistance; takes 4–8 weeks. |\n| **GeneXpert MTB/RIF (CBNAAT)** | All presumptive TB cases (India policy) | Rapid molecular test; detects MTB and rifampicin resistance within 2 hours. |\n| **Chest X-Ray (CXR)** | Contact tracing, initial clinical assessment | Upper lobe infiltrates, cavitation, hilar lymphadenopathy. |\n| **Mantoux Test** | Children, contacts, suspected latent TB | As described above. |\n| **TRUNAT (Line Probe Assay)** | Drug resistance testing | Detects resistance to INH and Rifampicin quickly. |\n\n---\n\n### D) PREVENTION AND CONTROL (ANTI-TUBERCULAR THERAPY)\n\n**1. Vaccination**:\n* **BCG (Bacillus Calmette-Guérin)**: Given at birth (0.1 ml intradermal in left upper arm). Provides 80% protection against severe forms of childhood TB (miliary TB, TB meningitis). Does NOT significantly prevent adult pulmonary TB.\n\n**2. NTEP Treatment Regimens (National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme)**:\n* **Intensive Phase (IP)**: 2 months — Isoniazid (H) + Rifampicin (R) + Pyrazinamide (Z) + Ethambutol (E) → **2HRZE**\n* **Continuation Phase (CP)**: 4 months — Isoniazid (H) + Rifampicin (R) → **4HR**\n* **DOTS (Directly Observed Treatment — Short Course)**: The cornerstone of the NTEP. A health worker directly observes the patient swallowing every dose of anti-TB drugs.\n* **Drug-Resistant TB (DR-TB)**: Treated with longer regimens (bedaquiline, delamanid, linezolid).\n\n**3. Contact Tracing**: All household contacts of sputum-positive (smear +) TB patients are screened for TB symptoms.\n\n**4. Nikshay Poshan Yojna**: Direct Benefit Transfer of ₹500/month to notified TB patients for nutritional support during the entire treatment duration.\n\n**5. National Goal**: India aims for **TB-free status by 2025**, 5 years ahead of the WHO End TB Strategy (2030).",
