    "text": "Describe POLIOMYELITIS: A) Epidemiology B)Diagnosis And Clinical Presentation C) Management Vaccines For Poliomyelitis (OPV &IPV) {Study In Conjugation With National Polio Surveillance Program [NPSP]}",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### POLIOMYELITIS (INFANTILE PARALYSIS)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: Poliovirus — types 1, 2, 3 (Type 1 is most common cause of paralytic polio; type 2 has been eradicated globally as of 2015).\n* **Host**: Children under 5 years are most susceptible. Adults can be infected.\n* **Transmission**: Feco-oral route (contaminated water, food) and oropharyngeal route.\n* **Incubation Period**: 7–14 days (range 3–35 days).\n* **Infectivity**: Pharyngeal secretions for 1 week; feces for 3–6 weeks.\n\n### B) DIAGNOSIS & CLINICAL PRESENTATION\n* **Clinical Types**:\n  1. *Inapparent (Subclinical) Infection (90–95%)*: No symptoms, but antibodies develop. (Iceberg phenomenon)\n  2. *Abortive Poliomyelitis (4–5%)*: Minor illness — fever, sore throat, headache, vomiting. Resolves fully.\n  3. *Non-paralytic Poliomyelitis*: Aseptic meningitis — neck and back stiffness, without paralysis.\n  4. *Paralytic Poliomyelitis (<1%)*: Flaccid, asymmetrical weakness of limbs (usually legs); lower motor neuron signs.\n     * **Spinal Type**: Most common; affects anterior horn cells of spinal cord.\n     * **Bulbar Type**: Affects brainstem; life-threatening if respiratory muscles involved.\n     * **Post-Polio Syndrome**: Reappearance of weakness 15–40 years after original infection.\n* **Diagnosis**: Isolation of virus from stool (2 samples within 14 days of onset of paralysis) — the **gold standard** for surveillance.\n\n### C) PREVENTION AND CONTROL\n* **Oral Polio Vaccine (OPV — Sabin vaccine)**: Live attenuated, 2 drops orally.\n  * Provides both individual and **community (herd) immunity** by excreting attenuated virus in stools.\n  * Risk of **Vaccine-Associated Paralytic Polio (VAPP)**: 1 per 4 million doses.\n* **Inactivated Polio Vaccine (IPV — Salk vaccine)**: Killed virus, given intramuscularly.\n  * No risk of VAPP. Added to Indian national schedule (1 fIPV dose at 14 weeks).\n* **National Pulse Polio Immunization (PPI)**: Mass OPV given on National Immunization Days (NIDs) to all children under 5 years, regardless of prior vaccination status.\n* **AFP (Acute Flaccid Paralysis) Surveillance**: All cases of AFP in children <15 years must be reported within 24 hours and stool samples collected for poliovirus isolation.\n* **India was certified polio-free in 2014** after 3 years without a wild poliovirus case.",
