    "text": "CHOLERA: A) Epidemiology B) Strategy Adopted For Control Of Cholera Outbreak In A Village C)Immunization ,Prevention And Control",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 7,
    "answer": "### CHOLERA\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: *Vibrio cholerae* (serogroups O1 — Classical and El Tor biotypes, and O139 Bengal).\n* **Host**: All ages. People with blood group O have highest susceptibility. Low gastric acid increases susceptibility.\n* **Reservoir**: Humans (cases and carriers); aquatic environments (river estuaries).\n* **Transmission**: Feco-oral route via contaminated water (most common) and food (flies, uncooked shellfish).\n* **Incubation Period**: Few hours to 5 days (average 2–3 days).\n* **Clinical Features**: Sudden profuse, painless watery diarrhea (\"rice-water stools\"), vomiting, rapid severe dehydration, electrolyte imbalance, muscle cramps. Death can occur within hours from dehydration shock.\n\n### B) STRATEGY ADOPTED FOR CONTROL OF CHOLERA OUTBREAK\n* **Immediate notification**: Cholera is a **notifiable disease** under the IHR (International Health Regulations); must be reported to WHO immediately.\n* **Case Management**:\n  * Mild-Moderate: **Oral Rehydration Solution (ORS)** — WHO low-osmolarity ORS formula.\n  * Severe: IV fluids — **Ringer's Lactate** (preferred); followed by antibiotics (**Doxycycline** 300 mg single dose for adults).\n* **Source Control**: Identify and chlorinate the contaminated water source. Collect water samples for laboratory confirmation (stool culture with hanging drop microscopy or TCBS agar).\n* **Contact Tracing**: Identify all household contacts and food handlers; chemoprophylaxis with Doxycycline.\n* **Environmental Sanitation**: Prevent open defecation; ensure sanitary excreta disposal; fly control.\n* **Health Education**: Boiling drinking water, hand hygiene, avoiding street food during outbreaks.\n\n### C) PREVENTION AND CONTROL\n* **Vaccines**: Oral Cholera Vaccines (OCV):\n  * *Dukoral* (WHO-certified): Inactivated whole-cell + recombinant B-subunit vaccine; 2 doses.\n  * *Shanchol* (Indian-manufactured): 2 doses; used in outbreak response and high-risk areas.\n* **Water and Sanitation**: The most effective and long-term method of cholera prevention.\n* **Notification and Surveillance**: Under the IDSP (Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme).",
