    "text": "Typhoid Fever : A)Epidemiology B) Laboratory Diagnosis C)Immunization , Prevention And Control.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 6,
    "answer": "### TYPHOID FEVER (ENTERIC FEVER)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: *Salmonella typhi* (causative of typhoid) and *S. paratyphi A, B, C* (causative of paratyphoid).\n* **Host**: All ages; children and young adults most affected. Long-term carriers (especially gallbladder carriers) are the main reservoir.\n* **Transmission**: Feco-oral route via contaminated food and water (\"5 Fs\" — Food, Fingers, Flies, Fomites, Fluids).\n* **Incubation Period**: 10–14 days (range 3–60 days).\n* **Characteristics**: Sustained bacteremia, macrophage invasion, lymph node enlargement (Peyer's patches), rose spots, hepatosplenomegaly.\n\n### B) LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS\n| Test | Timing | Details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Blood Culture** | 1st week | Gold standard; 80–90% positive in the first week |\n| **Bone Marrow Culture** | Anytime | Most sensitive test (90–95%); useful even after antibiotic therapy |\n| **Widal Test** | 2nd week | Agglutination test; significant if O titer ≥1:160, H titer ≥1:320 |\n| **Stool Culture** | 3rd–4th week | Also useful for identifying chronic carriers |\n| **Urine Culture** | 3rd week | Less sensitive |\n| **Typhidot (IgM ELISA)** | Early | Rapid card test detecting specific IgM antibodies |\n\n### C) IMMUNIZATION & PREVENTION\n* **Vaccines**:\n  * *Ty21a (Vivotif)*: Live oral vaccine; 3–4 capsules on alternate days; not for children <6 years.\n  * *Vi Capsular Polysaccharide (Vi CPS)*: Single intramuscular injection; protection for 3 years.\n  * *Typbar-TCV (Typhoid Conjugate Vaccine)*: Latest WHO-prequalified conjugate vaccine; single injection; long-lasting immunity; recommended from 6 months of age. Now part of India's routine immunization schedule.\n* **Prevention**: Safe water supply, proper sewage disposal, food hygiene, hand washing, and identification/treatment of carriers (long-term Ampicillin or Ciprofloxacin for carriers).\n\n### D) TREATMENT\n* First-line: **Ceftriaxone** IV or **Azithromycin** oral for 7–10 days.\n* For MDR strains: **Azithromycin** or **Carbapenems**.",
    "page": 18
