    "text": "Describe HEPATITIS B: A)Problem Statement B) Epidemiology C) Immunization,Prevention And Control Or Containment Of The Infection. (Hepb Vaccine Schedule)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 5,
    "answer": "### HEPATITIS B\n\n### A) PROBLEM STATEMENT\nHepatitis B is a major global public health problem. WHO estimates 296 million people worldwide are living with chronic Hepatitis B infection. It is a leading cause of liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC).\n\n### B) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: Hepatitis B Virus (HBV) — double-stranded DNA virus (Hepadnaviridae family).\n* **Key Serological Markers**:\n  * *HBsAg (Surface Antigen)*: First marker to appear; indicates current active infection.\n  * *Anti-HBs*: Appears after recovery; indicates immunity (also seen post-vaccination).\n  * *HBeAg*: Marker of high viral replication and high infectivity.\n  * *Anti-HBc IgM*: Indicates recent acute infection.\n* **Transmission**:\n  1. **Perinatal (Mother to child)**: Most common mode in high-endemic areas (e.g., Asia). Occurs at birth.\n  2. **Sexual transmission**: Unprotected sex with infected person.\n  3. **Parenteral**: Contaminated blood transfusions, IV drug use (shared needles), needle-stick injuries in healthcare workers.\n* **Natural History**: ~90% of adults recover fully; ~5–10% develop chronic infection. Of chronic carriers, 20–30% develop cirrhosis or HCC over decades.\n\n### C) IMMUNIZATION & PREVENTION\n* **Hepatitis B Vaccine** (Recombinant HBsAg):\n  * 3-dose schedule at 0, 1, 6 months (or 0, 1, 2 months in accelerated schedule).\n  * **Birth dose** (within 24 hours) is critical to prevent perinatal transmission.\n  * Under India's Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), Hepatitis B is given as part of **Pentavalent vaccine** (DPT + HepB + Hib) at 6, 10, 14 weeks.\n* **Post-exposure Prophylaxis**: Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin (HBIG) + vaccine within 12 hours of exposure (for newborns of HBsAg-positive mothers and needle-stick victims).\n* **Screening of Blood Donors**: Mandatory HBsAg testing of all blood donations.\n* **Universal Precautions**: Gloves, safe needle disposal, no needle re-use.",
