    "text": "LYMPHATIC FILARIASIS:  A) Epidemiology B)Filarial Detection Tests C)Add A Note On Filarial Indices D) Prevention And Control                            {Study In Conjugation With Nvbdcb}",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 6,
    "answer": "### LYMPHATIC FILARIASIS (ELEPHANTIASIS)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: *Wuchereria bancrofti* (most common in India), *Brugia malayi*, *Brugia timori*.\n* **Vector**: *Culex quinquefasciatus* (night-biting mosquito) — breeds in organically polluted, stagnant dirty water (drains, septic tanks).\n* **Host**: Humans are the only definitive host. Adults in endemic areas with repeated exposure.\n* **Distribution in India**: Highly endemic in eastern and southern states — Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Odisha, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu.\n\n### B) FILARIAL DETECTION TESTS\n* **Night Blood Survey (Microfilaria Survey)**:\n  * Blood collected between **10 PM and 2 AM** (nocturnal periodicity of microfilariae).\n  * Thick blood smear stained with Giemsa/hematoxylin to identify microfilariae.\n* **DEC Provocation Test (Daytime detection)**: 100 mg of DEC given orally at noon; blood collected 30–60 minutes later. Causes microfilariae to temporarily migrate to peripheral blood.\n* **Filarial Antigen Detection Tests (ICT card test)**: Immunochromatographic test detecting *W. bancrofti* circulating antigen. Can be done anytime (no nocturnal restriction).\n* **Ultrasound**: Can detect living adult worms in lymphatics (\"filarial dance sign\").\n\n### C) NIGHT BLOOD SURVEY INDICES\n* **Microfilaria Rate (MFR)**: Percentage of persons positive for microfilariae in the survey.\n* **Average Microfilaria Density**: Mean count of microfilariae per 20 mm³ of blood.\n* **Endemicity**: MFR > 1% = endemic area; MFR > 5% = highly endemic.\n\n### D) PREVENTION & CONTROL\n* **Mass Drug Administration (MDA)**: Annual single-dose **DEC (6 mg/kg) + Albendazole (400 mg)** given to entire at-risk population (excluding pregnant women and those <2 years).\n* **Vector Control**: Eliminating *Culex* breeding in dirty drains, proper sewage management.\n* **Morbidity Management**: Foot hygiene, lymphoedema physiotherapy, hydrocele surgery.\n* **National Programme**: National Filaria Control Programme (NFCP) / under NVBDCP.",
