    "text": "JAPANESE ENCEPHALITIS: A)Epidemiology B)Prevention And Control. C)JE Vaccine(3marks) { NVBDCP}",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 7,
    "answer": "### JAPANESE ENCEPHALITIS (JE)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: Japanese Encephalitis Virus (JEV) — single-stranded RNA Flavivirus.\n* **Vector**: *Culex tritaeniorhynchus* mosquito (breeds in rice paddies; bites between dusk and dawn).\n* **Reservoir Host**: **Pigs** (amplifying host — virus multiplies to high titres); **Ardeid birds** (herons, egrets — maintain the natural cycle).\n* **Dead-end Hosts**: Humans and horses (do not develop high enough viraemia to infect mosquitoes further).\n* **Distribution**: Highly endemic in North-Eastern India, UP, Bihar, West Bengal, Tamil Nadu, Odisha. Peaks during and after monsoon.\n\n### B) PREVENTION AND CONTROL\n* **Vector Control**: Controlling *Culex* mosquitoes; reducing contact with breeding sites (rice paddies near pig farms).\n* **Pig Vaccination**: Mass vaccination of pigs in endemic areas to break the transmission cycle.\n* **Environmental Measures**: Avoiding pig farms near human habitations.\n\n### C) JE VACCINE (TYPES AND SCHEDULES)\n* **Killed Mouse Brain-Derived Vaccine** (older, not used in India now): 3 doses on days 0, 7, 28.\n* **SA-14-14-2 Live Attenuated Vaccine (Chinese vaccine — now used in India)**:\n  * Brand: *JENVAC* (indigenously manufactured by Bharat Biotech).\n  * **Schedule**: 2 doses — first dose at **1–1.5 years**, booster at **2 years** of age.\n  * **Route**: Subcutaneous injection.\n  * Integrated into India's routine immunization schedule in endemic districts.\n* **Case Fatality Rate**: ~25–30% of clinical cases.\n* **Sequelae**: 30–50% of survivors have permanent neurological disabilities (epilepsy, cognitive impairment, paralysis).",
    "page": 20
