    "text": "KALA AZAR (Leshmaniasis)Under The Following Headings: A) Epidemiology B)Types & Clinical Features C) Prevention And Control.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### KALA AZAR (VISCERAL LEISHMANIASIS)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: *Leishmania donovani* (protozoa; intracellular parasite of reticuloendothelial cells).\n* **Vector**: Female **Sandfly** (*Phlebotomus argentipes*) — bites at dusk and dawn; breeds in damp mud floors.\n* **Reservoir**: Humans are the only significant reservoir in India (anthroponotic cycle).\n* **Distribution**: Hyper-endemic in Bihar (especially Muzaffarpur), UP, West Bengal, and Jharkhand in India.\n* **Incubation Period**: 2–6 months (range: weeks to years).\n\n### B) TYPES & CLINICAL FEATURES\n1. **Visceral Leishmaniasis (Kala Azar)**: Progressive, prolonged fever (weeks/months), massive splenomegaly + hepatomegaly, weight loss, weakness, darkening of skin (\"Kala Azar\" = Black Fever in Hindi), anaemia, leukopenia (pancytopenia).\n2. **Post-Kala-Azar Dermal Leishmaniasis (PKDL)**: Skin rash appearing months to years after apparently successful treatment of kala azar. Lesions are hypopigmented macules progressing to nodules. Acts as a **reservoir** for continued transmission.\n\n### C) DIAGNOSIS\n* **Definitive**: Demonstration of *Leishmania* (LD bodies) in spleen/bone marrow/lymph node aspirate (gold standard — very sensitive).\n* **Serological**: **rK39 Immunochromatographic Test** — rapid, highly sensitive and specific card test; used in field settings.\n* **Leishmanin (Montenegro) Test**: Not useful in active disease (negative); useful post-treatment to assess immune response.\n\n### D) TREATMENT & CONTROL\n* **First-line Drug**: **Liposomal Amphotericin B** (single IV infusion; highly effective, low toxicity).\n* **Alternative**: **Miltefosine** (oral; first oral drug for VL).\n* **Vector Control**: Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) with DDT/Synthetic pyrethroids on walls.\n* **Case Detection & Treatment**: Active surveillance using rK39 tests in endemic villages.\n* **National Programme**: NVBDCP — Kala-Azar Elimination Programme. **Target: Elimination (<1 case/10,000 population at block level) by 2023**.",
