    "text": "Describe RABIES (hydrophobia): A) Epidemiology B) Add A Note On Wound Treatment. C) Pre Exposure And Post Exposure Prophylaxis . D)Vaccination For Rabies",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### RABIES (HYDROPHOBIA)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: Rabies Lyssavirus — bullet-shaped, single-stranded RNA virus (Rhabdoviridae).\n* **Reservoir**: **Domestic dogs** are the primary reservoir and source of human infection in India (95%+ of cases). Also bats, foxes, jackals, cats.\n* **Transmission**: Bite of a rabid animal. Rarely: scratches, licks on open wounds/mucous membranes (no known aerosol/human-to-human transmission).\n* **Incubation Period**: Typically 2–8 weeks (can range from 10 days to 2 years). Shorter if bite is on the face/neck (near brain).\n* **Case Fatality**: Once clinical symptoms appear — **100% fatal**. No treatment exists for clinical rabies.\n\n### B) WOUND TREATMENT (CRITICAL — START IMMEDIATELY)\n1. **Immediate thorough washing** of the bite wound with soap and running water for **minimum 15 minutes**.\n2. **Chemical disinfection**: Apply 70% ethanol, povidone-iodine, or benzalkonium chloride.\n3. **Wound management**: Avoid suturing if possible; if necessary, suture loosely after RIG instillation. Tetanus toxoid if indicated.\n\n### C) POST-EXPOSURE PROPHYLAXIS (PEP)\n**WHO Bite Category Classification**:\n| Category | Type of Exposure | Treatment |\n|---|---|---|\n| **I** | Touching/feeding animals; licks on intact skin | None required |\n| **II** | Minor scratches/abrasion; licks on broken skin | Wound washing + **Vaccine** only |\n| **III** | Single or multiple transdermal bites; exposure to bat bites; licks on mucous membranes | Wound washing + **Vaccine + RIG (Rabies Immunoglobulin)** |\n\n* **Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV)** — Cell culture vaccines (Vero cell, PCEC):\n  * Standard schedule: Days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28 (5 doses, IM in deltoid).\n  * **Essen Regimen** (4-dose WHO 2018): Days 0, 3, 7, 14.\n* **Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG)**: Given for Category III only; infiltrated into and around the wound site on Day 0 only.\n  * *Human RIG (HRIG)*: 20 IU/kg body weight.\n  * *Equine RIG (ERIG)*: 40 IU/kg body weight (cheaper; skin test required).\n\n### D) PREVENTION\n* **Animal vaccination**: Mass vaccination of dogs in urban areas.\n* **Pre-exposure Prophylaxis (PreP)**: 3 doses (Days 0, 7, 21/28) for high-risk individuals — veterinarians, animal handlers, wildlife workers.\n* **National Rabies Control Programme (NRCP)** under NHM.",
