    "text": "LEPROSY: A) Epidemiology B)Classification Of Leprosy C) Lepromin Test D) Clinical Features &Disability Limitation In Leprosy E) Prevention And Control { Study In Detail Along With National Leprosy Eradication  Program ( Nlep)}",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 6,
    "answer": "### LEPROSY (HANSEN'S DISEASE)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: *Mycobacterium leprae* — acid-fast bacillus; obligate intracellular parasite with very long doubling time (~12 days).\n* **Host**: All ages; prolonged close contact required. Not all exposed individuals develop disease (genetic susceptibility plays a role).\n* **Transmission**: Droplet infection from nasal secretions of **Multibacillary (MB)** untreated cases (main route); less commonly through skin.\n* **Incubation Period**: 2–5 years (Tuberculoid type) to 8–12 years (Lepromatous type).\n* **Infectivity**: Only untreated MB cases (BL, LL types) are infectious; PB patients are non-infectious.\n* **India's Status**: India declared leprosy \"eliminated\" in 2005 (prevalence <1/10,000 population) but cases still occur.\n\n### B) CLASSIFICATION OF LEPROSY (Ridley-Jopling & WHO Classification)\n\n**WHO Classification (for MDT)**:\n| Type | Skin Lesions | Smear | MDT Duration |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Paucibacillary (PB)** | 1–5 lesions | Negative | 6 months |\n| **Multibacillary (MB)** | >5 lesions | Positive/Negative | 12 months |\n\n**Ridley-Jopling Immunological Spectrum**:\n* TT (Tuberculoid) → BT → BB → BL → LL (Lepromatous) — based on host immune response.\n\n### C) LEPROMIN TEST\n* **Principle**: Intradermal injection of lepromin (killed *M. leprae* suspension); measures cell-mediated immunity (CMI) response.\n* **Reading**:\n  * *Fernandez Reaction (48 hours)*: Erythema/induration — indicates prior sensitisation.\n  * *Mitsuda Reaction (3–4 weeks)*: Nodule/ulceration ≥5 mm = **Positive** (strong CMI — Tuberculoid type).\n* **Significance**: Negative Lepromin = Lepromatous end (poor CMI, high infectivity).\n* **Note**: Not a diagnostic test — used for **classification and prognosis only**.\n\n### D) CLINICAL FEATURES\n* **Tuberculoid (TT)**: Single/few well-defined hypopigmented, anaesthetic skin patches; peripheral nerve thickening.\n* **Lepromatous (LL)**: Numerous poorly demarcated, bilateral, symmetric lesions; eyebrow loss (madarosis); leonine facies; nasal collapse.\n* **Reactions**: Lepra Reactions (Type 1/Reversal, Type 2/ENL) — immune-mediated inflammatory episodes.\n\n### E) TREATMENT — MULTI-DRUG THERAPY (MDT) (WHO)\n* **PB Leprosy**: Rifampicin 600 mg (monthly, supervised) + Dapsone 100 mg (daily, self-administered) × **6 months**.\n* **MB Leprosy**: Rifampicin 600 mg + Clofazimine 300 mg (monthly, supervised) + Dapsone 100 mg + Clofazimine 50 mg (daily, self-administered) × **12 months**.",
