    "text": "INFUENZA: A) Epidemiology B)Antigenic Shift Vs Antigenic Drift C)Prevention And Control(vaccines for influenza)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### INFLUENZA\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Agent**: Influenza virus (Orthomyxovirus) — types A, B, C. Type A causes pandemics; Type B causes epidemics; Type C causes mild illness only.\n* **Genome**: Segmented, single-stranded RNA (8 segments) — this segmented nature allows genetic reassortment.\n* **Surface Antigens**: **Haemagglutinin (H)** — responsible for viral attachment to host cells; **Neuraminidase (N)** — responsible for viral release. Currently 18 H subtypes and 11 N subtypes (H1–H18, N1–N11).\n* **Host**: All ages; high-risk groups are elderly (>65), pregnant women, immunocompromised, and persons with chronic diseases.\n* **Transmission**: Respiratory droplets (short range) and contact with contaminated surfaces; incubation 1–4 days.\n\n### B) ANTIGENIC SHIFT VS ANTIGENIC DRIFT\n| Parameter | Antigenic Drift | Antigenic Shift |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Definition** | Minor, gradual, cumulative changes in H or N antigens due to random point mutations in the viral genome | Abrupt, major change in H and/or N antigens — a completely new combination appears |\n| **Mechanism** | Point mutations during viral replication (no reassortment) | **Genetic reassortment** between two different Influenza A viruses in a co-infected host (e.g., pigs) |\n| **Result** | Annual seasonal epidemics (partial immunity in population) | Pandemic potential — population has no prior immunity to the new subtype |\n| **Example** | Seasonal H3N2 drift variants | H1N1 pandemic (Spanish Flu 1918, Swine Flu 2009) |\n\n### C) PREVENTION AND CONTROL\n* **Influenza Vaccine**: Annual vaccination with **trivalent or quadrivalent inactivated influenza vaccine (TIV/QIV)**. Recommended annually for high-risk groups.\n  * Composition updated every year by WHO to match predicted circulating strains.\n* **Antiviral Treatment**: **Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)** 75 mg BD × 5 days — effective if started within **48 hours** of symptom onset.\n* **Non-pharmaceutical Interventions**: Hand hygiene, respiratory etiquette (cover cough), wearing masks, avoiding crowded places during outbreaks, school closures during pandemics.",
