    "text": "Classify Arboviral Infections.Add A Note On Epidemiological Features,  Prevention And Control Of KFD(KYASANUR FOREST DISEASE)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Arboviral Infections and Kyasanur Forest Disease (KFD)\n\nArboviral (arthropod-borne) infections are diseases transmitted to humans and other vertebrates by the bite of hematophagous arthropods (mosquitoes, ticks, sandflies).\n\n#### 1. Classification of Arboviral Infections\nArboviruses are classified immunologically and molecularly into four primary families:\n1.  **Flaviviridae (Flaviviruses):**\n    *   *Examples:* Dengue Fever, Japanese Encephalitis (JE), Kyasanur Forest Disease (KFD), Yellow Fever, West Nile Fever.\n    *   *Vectors:* Mosquitoes (*Aedes*, *Culex*), Ticks (*Haemaphysalis*).\n2.  **Togaviridae (Alphaviruses):**\n    *   *Examples:* Chikungunya Fever, Eastern/Western Equine Encephalitis.\n    *   *Vector:* Mosquitoes (*Aedes*, *Culex*).\n3.  **Bunyaviridae:**\n    *   *Examples:* Crimean-Congo Hemorrhagic Fever (CCHF), Sandfly Fever (Pappataci fever), Hantavirus.\n    *   *Vectors:* Ticks, Sandflies (*Phlebotomus*).\n4.  **Reoviridae (Coltiviruses/Orbiviruses):**\n    *   *Example:* Colorado Tick Fever.\n    *   *Vector:* Ticks.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Kyasanur Forest Disease (KFD)\nKyasanur Forest Disease (KFD), also known as \"Monkey Fever,\" is a tick-borne viral hemorrhagic fever endemic to the Western Ghats region of India.\n\n##### A. Epidemiological Features\n*   **Agent:** KFD Virus (KFDV), a single-stranded RNA virus belonging to the family *Flaviviridae*.\n*   **Host/Reservoir System:**\n    *   *Enzootic Reservoirs:* Wild rodents (e.g., forest rats, shrews), squirrels, and ground-dwelling birds.\n    *   *Amplifying Hosts:* Monkeys (black-faced Langur *Presbytis entellus* and Bonnet Macaque *Macaca radiata*). High monkey mortality in a forest is a highly sensitive **sentinel indicator** of viral activity.\n    *   *Incidental Hosts:* Humans and cattle (cattle do not develop high viremia but play a major role in feeding adult ticks).\n*   **Vector:** Hard ticks, primarily **_Haemaphysalis spinigera_**. Both trans-ovarial and trans-stadial transmission occur in ticks.\n*   **Transmission:** Transmission to humans occurs via the bite of infected tick nymphs (which are highly active on the forest floor) or through direct contact with an infected, dying, or dead monkey carcass. There is **no person-to-person transmission**.\n*   **Seasonality & Risk Groups:** Cases occur during the dry post-monsoon months (January to June), coinciding with the peak nymphal activity. Forest workers, fuel-wood gatherers, and tribal populations are at highest risk.\n*   **Geographical Distribution:** Originally discovered in the Sagar-Sorab area of Shimoga district (Karnataka) in 1957. It has now spread to other parts of Karnataka, Kerala (Wayanad), Tamil Nadu (Nilgiris), Goa, and Maharashtra.\n\n##### B. Prevention and Control\n1.  **Vaccination:**\n    *   An **inactivated (formalin-killed) KFD vaccine** is used in endemic areas for individuals aged 7–65 years living within 5 km of an active focus.\n    *   *Schedule:* 2 primary doses given at an interval of 1 month, followed by a booster dose at 6–9 months, and annual boosters for 5 consecutive years.\n2.  **Vector Control:**\n    *   **Chemical Spraying:** Dusting of insecticide (e.g., Pyrethroids or older Malathion) in forest patches where monkey deaths are reported (within a 50-meter radius of the carcass).\n    *   **Cattle dipping:** Applying acaricides to cattle to reduce the tick population.\n3.  **Personal Protection:**\n    *   Use of chemical tick repellents (e.g., Diethyltoluamide - DEET, or Dimethyl Phthalate - DMP) on exposed skin before entering the forest.\n    *   Wearing protective, long-sleeved clothing with trousers tucked into boots to prevent tick attachment.\n    *   Conducting self-examinations for ticks immediately after returning from the forest.\n4.  **Surveillance & Education:**\n    *   Daily monitoring of monkey deaths. Forest departments and health authorities collaborate to report carcasses.\n    *   Public health education encouraging communities to avoid entering forest areas where recent monkey deaths have occurred.",
