    "id": 196,
    "text": "Describe Zoonoses With Examples.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### Zoonoses: Concepts, Classifications, and Examples\n\nAccording to the World Health Organization (WHO), **Zoonoses** are defined as \"those diseases and infections which are naturally transmitted between vertebrate animals and man.\" Zoonotic diseases represent more than 60% of all human infectious pathogens.\n\n#### 1. Classification of Zoonoses\n\n##### A. Based on the Direction of Transmission\n*   **Anthropozoonoses:** Diseases transmitted primarily from vertebrate animals to humans. Man is usually an accidental, dead-end host.\n    *   *Examples:* Rabies, Brucellosis, Hydatid disease, Bovine Tuberculosis, Plague, Anthrax.\n*   **Zooanthroponoses (Reverse Zoonoses):** Diseases transmitted primarily from humans to vertebrate animals.\n    *   *Examples:* Human Tuberculosis (*Mycobacterium tuberculosis*) transmitted to cattle; Amoebiasis (*Entamoeba histolytica*) transmitted to dogs.\n*   **Amphixenoses:** Infections that are maintained and regularly transmitted in either direction between humans and other vertebrates.\n    *   *Examples:* *Schistosoma japonicum* infections, Chagas disease (*Trypanosoma cruzi*).\n\n##### B. Based on the Lifecycle of the Pathogen (Ecosystem)\n*   **Direct Zoonoses:** The pathogen is perpetuated in a single vertebrate host species and transmitted to humans by direct contact, fomites, or a mechanical vector.\n    *   *Examples:* Rabies, Brucellosis, Leptospirosis, Trichinellosis.\n*   **Cyclozoonoses:** Require more than one vertebrate host species to complete the pathogen's developmental cycle. No invertebrate host is involved.\n    *   *Examples:* Taeniasis (*Taenia saginata* requires humans and cattle; *Taenia solium* requires humans and pigs), Echinococcosis/Hydatid disease (requires dogs and sheep/cattle/humans).\n*   **Metazoonoses:** Diseases that require both vertebrate hosts and invertebrate vectors (in which the agent multiplies, develops, or both) to complete the transmission cycle.\n    *   *Examples:* Plague (vector: rat flea), Scrub Typhus (vector: trombiculid mite), Leishmaniasis (vector: sandfly), Arboviral diseases (vectors: mosquitoes, ticks).\n*   **Saprozoonoses:** Require both a vertebrate host and a non-animal environmental development site, such as soil, organic matter, or plants, for the pathogen's growth or replication.\n    *   *Examples:* Tetanus (*Clostridium tetani* spores in soil), Histoplasmosis, Coccidioidomycosis, Cutaneous Larva Migrans.\n\n#### 2. Key Examples by Etiological Agent\n*   **Viral:** Rabies (Lyssavirus), Kyasanur Forest Disease (Flavivirus), Ebola, Avian Influenza, Hantavirus, Yellow Fever.\n*   **Bacterial:** Brucellosis (*Brucella abortus/melitensis*), Leptospirosis (*Leptospira interrogans*), Anthrax (*Bacillus anthracis*), Plague (*Yersinia pestis*), Bovine TB (*Mycobacterium bovis*).\n*   **Rickettsial:** Scrub Typhus (*Orientia tsutsugamushi*), Q Fever (*Coxiella burnetii*).\n*   **Parasitic/Protozoal:** Toxoplasmosis (*Toxoplasma gondii*), Hydatid Disease (*Echinococcus granulosus*), Neurocysticercosis (*Taenia solium*).",
    "page": 22
