    "id": 214,
    "text": "DPT VACCINE",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### The DPT Vaccine\n\nThe DPT vaccine is a trivalent combination vaccine designed to protect infants and children against three highly infectious and potentially fatal bacterial diseases: **Diphtheria, Pertussis (Whooping Cough), and Tetanus**.\n\n#### 1. Composition\nEach standard 0.5 mL dose of DPT contains:\n*   **Diphtheria Toxoid:** Purified toxoid (typically 20–30 Lf).\n*   **Pertussis Vaccine:** Killed whole-cell *Bordetella pertussis* bacilli (at least 4 International Units).\n*   **Tetanus Toxoid:** Purified toxoid (typically 5–10 Lf).\n*   **Adjuvant:** Aluminum Phosphate or Aluminum Hydroxide is added to enhance the immune response.\n*   **Preservative:** Thiomersal is commonly included in multi-dose vials.\n\n#### 2. Immunization Schedule (Under UIP India)\nIn the primary infancy series, DPT is administered as part of the **Pentavalent Vaccine** (DPT-HepB-Hib). However, standalone DPT is administered as booster doses:\n\n*   **Primary Series (as Pentavalent):** 3 doses given at **6 weeks, 10 weeks, and 14 weeks** of age.\n*   **DPT Booster 1:** Given at **16 to 24 months** of age.\n*   **DPT Booster 2:** Given at **5 to 6 years** of age.\n*   *(Note: Beyond 7 years of age, whole-cell pertussis is associated with higher side effects. Therefore, Td or Tdap vaccines are used for older children and adults).*\n\n#### 3. Administration Details\n*   **Dose:** **0.5 mL**.\n*   **Route:** **Deep Intramuscular (IM)** injection.\n*   **Site:**\n    *   *Infants (< 2 years):* **Anterolateral aspect of the mid-thigh** (deltoid is avoided due to small muscle mass).\n    *   *Older children (5–6 years):* **Deltoid muscle** of the upper arm.\n\n#### 4. Cold Chain and Storage\n*   Must be stored in the Ice-Lined Refrigerator (ILR) between **+2°C and +8°C**.\n*   **Warning: DO NOT FREEZE.** Freezing damages the aluminum adjuvant, causing it to precipitate, and completely destroys the potency of the pertussis component.\n*   **The Shake Test:** If freezing is suspected, the \"Shake Test\" is performed. If the vaccine settles rapidly, leaving a clear supernatant after shaking, it has been frozen and must be discarded.\n\n#### 5. Adverse Effects and Contraindications\n*   *Mild Reactions:* Local pain, redness, swelling at the injection site, and mild to moderate fever.\n*   *Severe Reactions (primarily attributed to the whole-cell pertussis component):*\n    *   High fever ($> 40.5^\\circ\\text{C}$).\n    *   Persistent, inconsolable crying lasting $\\ge 3\\text{ hours}$.\n    *   Febrile seizures/convulsions.\n    *   Encephalopathy (altered consciousness or focal neurological signs) within 7 days of vaccination.\n*   *Absolute Contraindications:* History of severe anaphylaxis or encephalopathy within 7 days of a previous dose of DPT. In these cases, DT (Diphtheria-Tetanus) or acellular pertussis (aP) formulations should be substituted.",
    "page": 23
