    "text": "Describe HYPERTENSION under the following headings: a) Epidemiology b) AHA guidelines for BP classification. c)Risk Factors for HTN d)Rule of halves (3marks) e)Tracking of blood pressure(3marks) f) Prevention and control of HT.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Epidemiology of Non-Communicable Diseases",
    "stars": 10,
    "answer": "### HYPERTENSION (HIGH BLOOD PRESSURE)\n\n### A) EPIDEMIOLOGY\n* **Definition (JNC 7)**: Persistent elevation of systolic BP ≥140 mm Hg and/or diastolic BP ≥90 mm Hg.\n* **Prevalence**: ~25–30% of Indian adults are hypertensive; increasing rapidly with urbanisation and lifestyle changes.\n* **Risk Factors**: \n  * *Non-modifiable*: Age (progressive arterial stiffness), male gender, positive family history, genetic susceptibility.\n  * *Modifiable*: High salt intake (>5 g/day), obesity, physical inactivity, alcohol, smoking, chronic stress, diabetes.\n\n### B) AHA/ACC CLASSIFICATION (2017)\n| Category | Systolic (mm Hg) | | Diastolic (mm Hg) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Normal | <120 | AND | <80 |\n| Elevated | 120–129 | AND | <80 |\n| Stage 1 HTN | 130–139 | OR | 80–89 |\n| Stage 2 HTN | ≥140 | OR | ≥90 |\n\n### C) RULE OF HALVES (Park's SPM)\nDescribes the characteristic management gap in hypertension:\n* **Only 50%** of hypertensives in the community are **diagnosed**.\n* Of those diagnosed, only **50%** are on **treatment**.\n* Of those treated, only **50%** have their BP **adequately controlled**.\n* Net result: Only **12.5%** of all hypertensives achieve good control.\n\n### D) PREVENTION AND CONTROL\n* **Primary Prevention (Lifestyle Modifications)**:\n  1. Salt restriction (<5 g/day) — **DASH Diet** (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension).\n  2. Weight reduction (each kg of weight loss reduces SBP ~1 mm Hg).\n  3. Regular aerobic exercise — 30 min/day, 5 days/week.\n  4. Quit smoking and reduce alcohol.\n  5. Stress management techniques.\n* **Secondary Prevention (Drug Therapy)**:\n  * First-line antihypertensives: **ACE inhibitors, ARBs, Thiazide diuretics, Calcium Channel Blockers, Beta-blockers**.\n  * Target: <130/80 mm Hg for high-risk patients; <140/90 mm Hg for general population.\n* **Hypertensive Emergency**: IV Labetalol, Nicardipine — reduce BP by ≤25% in 1 hour.\n\n### E) NATIONAL PROGRAMME\n* **India Hypertension Control Initiative (IHCI)** under NHM — standardised treatment protocol, free medicines at public facilities.",
