    "id": 234,
    "text": "Add a Note on AGE PYRAMIDS.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Demography and Family Planning",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### Population Age-Sex Pyramids\n\nAn age pyramid (or population pyramid) is a graphical representation of the age and sex distribution of a specific population. It consists of two back-to-back bar charts: **males on the left** and **females on the right**, plotted against 5-year age groups (with the youngest at the base and the oldest at the apex).\n\n#### 1. Core Structural Types of Age Pyramids\n\n##### A. Expansive (Progressive / Triangular) Pyramid\n*   **Shape:** Broad base and rapid, steep tapering toward the top.\n*   **Demographic Profile:** Indicates a high birth rate, high infant mortality, and low life expectancy. The population is young and growing rapidly.\n*   **Examples:** Developing nations, particularly in Sub-Saharan Africa (and historically, India).\n\n##### B. Constrictive (Regressive / Beehive) Pyramid\n*   **Shape:** Narrower at the base than in the middle (looks like a beehive or urn).\n*   **Demographic Profile:** Indicates a rapidly declining birth rate, low infant mortality, and high life expectancy. There is a high proportion of older adults.\n*   **Examples:** Developed nations with aging populations (e.g., Japan, Italy, Germany).\n\n##### C. Stationary (Stable / Bell-shaped) Pyramid\n*   **Shape:** Rectangular, with relatively equal widths across all age cohorts, tapering only at the very top.\n*   **Demographic Profile:** Indicates stable, low birth and death rates. Near-zero population growth.\n*   **Examples:** Stable developed nations (e.g., Sweden, France).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Public Health and Socioeconomic Significance\n*   **Dependency Ratio:** Allows epidemiologists to calculate the dependency ratio:\n    $$\\text{Dependency Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Population } (< 15 \\text{ years}) + \\text{Population } (> 64 \\text{ years})}{\\text{Working-age Population } (15\\text{ to }64 \\text{ years})} \\times 100$$\n*   **Healthcare Planning:**\n    *   A broad base (progressive) alerts planners to allocate resources for maternal and child health (MCH), pediatric services, and immunizations.\n    *   A narrow base with a wide top (regressive) dictates a shift toward geriatric care, chronic disease management (oncology, cardiology), and social security.\n*   **Demographic Dividend:** Identifies if a country has a bulge in the working-age cohort (15–59 years). This represents a window of economic growth if supported by employment and health policies.",
    "page": 26
