    "id": 239,
    "text": "Add a note OCPs (Oral Contraceptives Pills).(Indications,C/I,complications)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Demography and Family Planning",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### Combined Oral Contraceptive Pills (COCs)\n\nCombined Oral Contraceptive Pills (COCs), such as **Mala-N and Mala-D**, are highly popular, reversible spacing methods containing synthetic estrogen (Ethinyl Estradiol) and progestin (Levonorgestrel). They are taken daily for 21 consecutive days, starting on the 1st to 5th day of the menstrual cycle, followed by 7 days of placebo or iron tablets (ferrous fumarate).\n\n#### 1. Indications\n*   **Contraception:** Spacing of pregnancies in women of reproductive age seeking highly reliable birth control.\n*   **Non-contraceptive Medical Uses:**\n    *   Treatment of moderate to severe dysmenorrhea.\n    *   Management of menorrhagia (excessive menstrual bleeding) and irregular cycles.\n    *   Management of Polycystic Ovarian Syndrome (PCOS) and associated acne/hirsutism.\n    *   Endometriosis symptom control.\n    *   Reduction of risk for ovarian and endometrial cancers.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Contraindications (C/I)\n\n##### A. Absolute Contraindications\n1.  **Thromboembolic Disorders:** History of Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT), Pulmonary Embolism (PE), stroke, or Coronary Artery Disease (CAD).\n2.  **Active Liver Disease:** Acute hepatitis, cirrhosis, or benign/malignant hepatic tumors.\n3.  **Hormone-Sensitive Malignancies:** Confirmed or suspected breast cancer or endometrial cancer.\n4.  **Pregnancy:** Known or suspected pregnancy.\n5.  **Age and Smoking:** Women aged $\\ge 35$ years who smoke $\\ge 15$ cigarettes/day (due to high cardiovascular risk).\n6.  **Undiagnosed Vaginal Bleeding.**\n\n##### B. Relative Contraindications\n1.  Uncontrolled moderate to severe hypertension.\n2.  Migraine headaches with aura (increased risk of stroke).\n3.  Diabetes mellitus with vascular complications.\n4.  Concurrent use of liver enzyme-inducing drugs (e.g., Rifampicin, Phenytoin, Phenobarbital) which accelerate OCP metabolism and cause contraceptive failure.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Complications and Side Effects\n\n##### A. Minor / Transient Side Effects\n*   Nausea and vomiting (usually subsides after the first few cycles).\n*   Breakthrough bleeding or spotting.\n*   Breast tenderness and mild weight gain (due to fluid retention).\n*   Chloasma (hyperpigmentation on the face).\n*   Mood swings and mild depression.\n\n##### B. Major / Long-term Complications\n*   **Venous Thromboembolism (VTE):** Estrogen increases hepatic synthesis of clotting factors, doubling the risk of DVT/PE.\n*   **Cardiovascular Events:** Small increase in the risk of myocardial infarction and ischemic stroke, particularly in women with pre-existing risk factors (hypertension, smoking).\n*   **Hepatobiliary Disease:** Increased risk of cholelithiasis (gallstones) and cholestatic jaundice.\n*   **Neoplasia:** Extremely small increase in the risk of cervical and breast cancers with long-term continuous use ($> 5$ years); however, they are highly protective against ovarian and endometrial cancers (risk reduced by 50%).",
