    "chapter": "Demography and Family Planning",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### **Classification of Contraceptive Methods**\n\nContraceptive methods are broadly divided into two major groups: **Spacing (Temporary) Methods** and **Terminal (Permanent) Methods**.\n\n#### **I. Spacing Methods (Temporary)**\nDesigned to space out births and are fully reversible upon discontinuation.\n\n1.  **Barrier Methods**:\n    *   **Physical**: Condoms (Male: polyurethane/latex; Female: Femidom), Vaginal Diaphragm, Vaginal Sponge (Today).\n    *   **Chemical**: Foams (aerosols), soluble pessaries, creams, jellies, and pastes (acting as spermicides; e.g., Nonoxynol-9).\n    *   **Combined**: Diaphragm used along with spermicidal jelly.\n2.  **Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)**:\n    *   **First Generation (Non-medicated)**: Lippes Loop (double-S shape, polyethylene).\n    *   **Second Generation (Copper-bearing)**: Copper T-200, Copper T-380A, Multiload-250, Multiload-375.\n    *   **Third Generation (Hormone-releasing)**: Progestasert (T-shaped, releases progesterone), Mirena (Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system: LNG-IUS, releases 20 mcg LNG daily).\n3.  **Hormonal Contraceptive Methods**:\n    *   **Oral Contraceptive Pills**:\n        *   *Combined Oral Pills*: Low-dose estrogen + progestogen (e.g., Mala-N, Mala-D).\n        *   *Progestogen-Only Pills (POPs / Minipill)*: Micro-dose progestogen daily.\n        *   *Once-a-Week Pill*: Non-steroidal Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulator (SERM) (e.g., Centchroman / Saheli / Chhaya).\n    *   **Injectable Contraceptives**:\n        *   *DMPA (Depot Medroxyprogesterone Acetate)*: 150 mg IM every 3 months (branded under National Program as *Antara*).\n        *   *NET-EN (Norethisterone Enanthate)*: 200 mg IM every 2 months.\n    *   **Subdermal Implants**:\n        *   *Norplant*: 6 levonorgestrel-containing capsules (effective for 5 years).\n        *   *Implanon/Nexplanon*: Single-rod progestogen implant (effective for 3 years).\n    *   **Emergency Contraceptives**: Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg single dose (within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse), Ulipristal acetate 30 mg.\n4.  **Natural / Fertility Awareness Methods**:\n    *   Calendar (Rhythm) Method.\n    *   Basal Body Temperature (BBT) Method.\n    *   Cervical Mucus (Billings) Method.\n    *   Symptothermal Method (combines BBT, mucus, and calendar).\n    *   Lactational Amenorrhea Method (LAM): Effective only for the first 6 months postpartum if mother is exclusively breastfeeding.\n    *   Coitus Interruptus (Withdrawal).\n\n#### **II. Terminal Methods (Permanent Sterilization)**\nDesigned for couples who have completed their family size.\n\n1.  **Male Sterilization (Vasectomy)**:\n    *   Conventional Vasectomy.\n    *   **No-Scalpel Vasectomy (NSV)**: Minimally invasive technique using dissecting forceps instead of a scalpel.\n2.  **Female Sterilization (Tubectomy)**:\n    *   **Minilaparotomy**: Small abdominal incision, most common postpartum method.\n    *   **Laparoscopy**: Visualizing fallopian tubes with a laparoscope and applying Silastic rings/clips.",
