    "text": "a)Describe CHILDHOOD MORTALITY (IMR,PNMR,NNMR,U5MR) b)What are the CAUSE INFANT MORTALITY in India.Describe the methods in reducing IMR.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "SPM in Obs, Paeds, and Geriatrics",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### **Childhood Mortality & Causes and Reduction of IMR**\n\n#### **a) Childhood Mortality Indicators**\nChildhood mortality indicators are vital statistics used to measure the health, socio-economic status, and healthcare accessibility of a community. The primary indicators defined by Park's textbook include:\n\n1.  **Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)**:\n    *   **Definition**: The number of deaths of infants under 1 year of age per 1,000 live births in a given year.\n    *   **Formula**:\n        $$\\text{IMR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of deaths under 1 year of age during the year}}{\\text{Total number of live births during the same year}} \\times 1,000$$\n    *   **Significance**: IMR is regarded as the single most sensitive indicator of the health status of a community and the level of socio-economic development.\n\n2.  **Perinatal Mortality Rate (PNMR)**:\n    *   **Definition**: Late fetal deaths (stillbirths after 28 weeks of gestation) plus early neonatal deaths (deaths within the first 7 days of life) per 1,000 total births.\n    *   **Formula**:\n        $$\\text{PNMR} = \\frac{\\text{Stillbirths (\\ge 28 weeks) + Early Neonatal Deaths (< 7 days) during the year}}{\\text{Total Live Births + Stillbirths during the same year}} \\times 1,000$$\n\n3.  **Neonatal Mortality Rate (NNMR)**:\n    *   **Definition**: The number of deaths of infants under 28 days of age per 1,000 live births in a given year.\n    *   **Formula**:\n        $$\\text{NNMR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of deaths under 28 days of age during the year}}{\\text{Total live births during the same year}} \\times 1,000$$\n    *   It is divided into *Early Neonatal Mortality* (deaths < 7 days) and *Late Neonatal Mortality* (deaths from 7 to 27 days).\n\n4.  **Under-5 Mortality Rate (U5MR)**:\n    *   **Definition**: The probability of a child dying between birth and exactly 5 years of age, expressed per 1,000 live births.\n    *   **Formula**:\n        $$\\text{U5MR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of deaths under 5 years of age during the year}}{\\text{Total live births during the same year}} \\times 1,000$$\n\n---\n\n#### **b) Causes of Infant Mortality in India**\nThe causes of infant mortality are categorized into direct (medical) causes and indirect (socio-economic) factors.\n\n##### **I. Direct/Medical Causes**\n1.  **Prematurity and Low Birth Weight (LBW)**: The leading cause, accounting for **35–40%** of all infant deaths (especially neonatal deaths).\n2.  **Acute Respiratory Infections (ARI)**: Primarily pneumonia, accounting for **15%** of infant deaths.\n3.  **Birth Asphyxia and Birth Trauma**: Accounts for **10–12%** of deaths, largely due to deliveries conducted by untrained personnel.\n4.  **Diarrheal Diseases**: Caused by poor hygiene, rotavirus, or bacterial infections, leading to fatal dehydration (**~10%**).\n5.  **Neonatal Sepsis and Infections**: Sepsis, meningitis, and omphalitis account for **~8–10%**.\n6.  **Congenital Anomalies**: Structural or genetic defects (**~6–8%**).\n\n##### **II. Indirect/Social Factors**\n*   **Low Maternal Literacy**: Ignorance of child care, hygiene, and nutrition.\n*   **Maternal Malnutrition and Anemia**: Leading to high rates of LBW babies.\n*   **Early Marriage and High Parity**: Young mothers and closely spaced pregnancies increase infant vulnerability.\n*   **Poor Sanitation and Unsafe Drinking Water**: Major driver of recurrent diarrhea and infections.\n*   **Gender Bias**: Higher mortality rates among female infants in some regions due to neglect.\n\n---\n\n#### **Methods for Reducing IMR in India**\nTo reduce IMR, a comprehensive multi-sectoral approach targeting pregnancy, delivery, and childhood is executed:\n\n1.  **High-Quality Antenatal Care (ANC)**:\n    *   Early registration of all pregnancies (within the first trimester).\n    *   At least 4 comprehensive ANC checkups.\n    *   Distribution of **Iron-Folic Acid (IFA)** tablets (100 mg elemental iron + 0.5 mg folic acid daily for 180 days) and Calcium supplementation to prevent maternal anemia and LBW.\n    *   Administration of **Tetanus Toxoid (TT) / Td** vaccine to prevent neonatal tetanus.\n2.  **Safe Delivery Practices (Intranatal Care)**:\n    *   Promotion of institutional delivery through cash incentives under **Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY)** and free treatment under **Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK)**.\n    *   Ensuring deliveries are conducted by **Skilled Birth Attendants (SBAs)** using clean delivery kits (the \"5 Cleans\": clean hands, clean surface, clean cord cut, clean cord tie, and clean cord stump).\n3.  **Essential Newborn Care (ENC)**:\n    *   Prevention of hypothermia using the \"warm chain\" and initiating **Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC)** for low-birth-weight babies.\n    *   Initiation of **breastfeeding within 1 hour** of birth, and ensuring **exclusive breastfeeding** for the first 6 months.\n    *   Hygienic cord care (chlorhexidine application) and avoiding pre-lacteal feeds.\n4.  **Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)**:\n    *   Full vaccination coverage against major vaccine-preventable diseases (BCG, Hep B, OPV, Rotavirus, Pentavalent, Measles-Rubella, PCV).\n5.  **Childhood Disease Management (IMNCI)**:\n    *   Implementing **Integrated Management of Neonatal and Childhood Illness (IMNCI)** guidelines at primary healthcare facilities.\n    *   Setting up ORS-Zinc corners for diarrheal management and ensuring immediate antibiotic therapy (Amoxicillin) for suspected pneumonia.\n6.  **Social Interventions**:\n    *   Enforcing laws against child marriage, promoting female education, encouraging spacing methods (e.g., postpartum IUCD), and improving clean water supply and sanitation under the Swachh Bharat Abhiyan.",
