    "text": "RMNCH+A under the following headings: a) Indicators b)Risk approach c)Targets and strategies d)Evaluation of MCH services.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "SPM in Obs, Paeds, and Geriatrics",
    "stars": 7,
    "answer": "### **RMNCH+A (Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child, and Adolescent Health)**\n\n#### **a) Key Indicators**\nIndicators under RMNCH+A are utilized to monitor program implementation and evaluate health outcomes across the continuum of care:\n\n*   **Impact Indicators**:\n    1.  **Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)**: Target is < 70 deaths per 100,000 live births.\n    2.  **Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)**: Target is < 25 deaths per 1,000 live births.\n    3.  **Under-5 Mortality Rate (U5MR)**: Target is < 32 deaths per 1,000 live births.\n    4.  **Total Fertility Rate (TFR)**: Target is 2.1 (replacement level fertility).\n*   **Outcome/Process Indicators**:\n    1.  **Antenatal Care (ANC) Coverage**: Percentage of pregnant women who had at least 4 ANC visits.\n    2.  **Institutional Delivery Rate**: Percentage of births occurring in healthcare facilities.\n    3.  **Full Immunization Coverage (FIC)**: Percentage of children fully vaccinated by age 1.\n    4.  **Unmet Need for Family Planning**: Percentage of women who want to space or limit births but are not using contraception.\n\n---\n\n#### **b) Risk Approach**\n*   **Concept**: \"Something for all, but more for those in need, in proportion to their need.\" It is a managerial strategy designed to optimize resource allocation by identifying high-risk mothers and infants early and prioritizing their care.\n*   **Identification of High-Risk Cases**:\n    *   *High-Risk Pregnancy*: Short stature (< 145 cm), age < 18 or > 35, severe anemia (Hb < 7 g/dL), pre-eclampsia/hypertension, previous C-section or postpartum hemorrhage, multiple gestation, diabetes.\n    *   *High-Risk Infant*: Birth weight < 2,000 grams, preterm (< 37 weeks), birth asphyxia, failure to breastfeed, congenital malformations.\n*   **Implementation**: ANMs and ASHAs screen for risk factors. Lower-risk cases are managed at sub-centres, while high-risk cases are referred to CHCs or First Referral Units (FRUs) for specialist care.\n\n---\n\n#### **c) Targets and Strategies**\n##### **Targets**\n*   Reduce MMR to < 70/100,000 live births, IMR to < 25/1,000 live births, and NNMR to < 16/1,000 live births.\n*   Increase institutional delivery to > 90% and full immunization coverage to > 90%.\n\n##### **Strategies (RMNCH+A 5x5 Matrix)**\n1.  **Reproductive Health**: Expanding spacing methods, promoting postpartum IUCD (PPIUCD), social marketing of condoms, and offering comprehensive abortion care.\n2.  **Maternal Health**: Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) for institutional delivery incentives, Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA) for specialist checkups on the 9th of every month, and the **LaQshya** initiative to improve labor room quality.\n3.  **Newborn Health**: Securing the \"warm chain,\" setting up Special Newborn Care Units (SNCUs) and Newborn Care Corners (NBCCs), and implementing Home-Based Newborn Care (HBNC) via ASHAs.\n4.  **Child Health**: Implementing the Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK) for early screening of the \"4 Ds\" (Defects, Deficiencies, Diseases, Development delays) and expanding Vitamin A and deworming coverage.\n5.  **Adolescent Health**: Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS), Peer Educator Programme, and setting up Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics (AFHCs).\n\n---\n\n#### **d) Evaluation of MCH Services**\nEvaluation of Maternal and Child Health (MCH) services involves measuring efficacy, accessibility, and quality:\n\n1.  **Quantitative Evaluation**: Using routine data from the **Health Management Information System (HMIS)** and large-scale household surveys (e.g., National Family Health Survey - NFHS) to calculate process rates (e.g., percentage of institutional deliveries, immunization rates) and impact rates (IMR, MMR).\n2.  **Qualitative Evaluation**: Auditing patient satisfaction, maternal death reviews (maternal death surveillance and response - MDSR through verbal autopsies), and monitoring structural facilities against Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS).",
