    "id": 257,
    "text": "Integrated Child Development Services(ICDS)-beneficiaries and Benefits",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "SPM in Obs, Paeds, and Geriatrics",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### **Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)**\n\n#### **1. Introduction**\nLaunched on **2nd October 1975**, the **Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)** scheme is one of the world's largest and most unique programmes for early childhood care and development, operating through a network of community centers called **Anganwadi Centres (AWCs)**.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Beneficiaries**\nICDS targets the most vulnerable segments of the population to ensure holistic development:\n1.  Children under 3 years of age.\n2.  Children aged 3 to 6 years.\n3.  Pregnant women.\n4.  Lactating mothers (up to 6 months postpartum).\n5.  Adolescent girls (aged 11–18 years in selected districts for nutritional support and life-skills education).\n\n---\n\n#### **3. Benefits and Services (The Package of 6 Services)**\nThe scheme provides an integrated package of services to synergize health, nutrition, and early education:\n\n1.  **Supplementary Nutrition (SNP)**:\n    *   Designed to bridge the gap between actual dietary intake and recommended dietary allowance (RDA) in the community. Provided for 300 days a year.\n    *   *Children (6 months to 72 months)*: 500 kcal of energy + 12–15g of protein.\n    *   *Severely Malnourished Children (6 months to 72 months)*: 800 kcal of energy + 20–25g of protein (provided as Special Nutrition).\n    *   *Pregnant & Lactating Mothers*: 600 kcal of energy + 18–20g of protein.\n2.  **Immunization**:\n    *   Vaccination of infants against vaccine-preventable diseases (BCG, OPV, DPT, Hepatitis B, Measles) and Tetanus Toxoid (TT/Td) vaccination for pregnant mothers, conducted in coordination with ANMs and ASHAs at the Anganwadi Centre during Village Health Sanitation and Nutrition Days (VHSND).\n3.  **Health Check-ups**:\n    *   Routine health monitoring, antenatal check-ups, postnatal checkups, treatment of minor ailments (diarrhea, skin infections), and regular recording of child weight and height on growth charts.\n4.  **Referral Services**:\n    *   Screening and identification of severely malnourished children, high-risk pregnant women, or sick infants, followed by their prompt referral to primary health centres (PHCs), sub-district hospitals, or Nutrition Rehabilitation Centres (NRCs).\n5.  **Non-formal Pre-school Education (PSE)**:\n    *   Provided to children aged 3–6 years. Focuses on play-based learning, cognitive growth, socialization, and preparation for primary school.\n6.  **Nutrition and Health Education (NHED)**:\n    *   Behavior-change communication targeted at women aged 15–45 years, particularly mothers. Covers breast-feeding guidelines, weaning, hygiene, sanitation, and home management of childhood diarrhea.",
