    "id": 258,
    "text": "Antenatal care-Objectives and Components",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "SPM in Obs, Paeds, and Geriatrics",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### **Antenatal Care (ANC)**\n\n#### **1. Objectives of Antenatal Care**\n**Antenatal Care** refers to the systemic medical supervision, care, and support provided to a pregnant woman from the time of conception up to the onset of labor. Its objectives are:\n\n1.  To promote, protect, and maintain the physical and mental health of the pregnant woman.\n2.  To detect \"high-risk\" pregnancies early and initiate specialized clinical management.\n3.  To foresee and prevent obstetrical complications (e.g., pre-eclampsia, hemorrhage, gestational diabetes).\n4.  To ensure the delivery of a healthy, full-term baby with minimal neonatal complications.\n5.  To educate and prepare the mother for safe childbirth, successful lactation, and postpartum contraception.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Components of Antenatal Care**\nA robust antenatal program comprises the following key components:\n\n1.  **Early Registration of Pregnancy**:\n    *   Mandatory registration of the pregnant woman at a sub-centre, PHC, or AWC within the first trimester (under 12 weeks of gestation).\n2.  **Scheduled Antenatal Visits**:\n    *   According to National Health Guidelines, a minimum of **4 antenatal visits** is required:\n        *   *1st Visit*: Within 12 weeks (for early registration and confirmation).\n        *   *2nd Visit*: Between 14 and 26 weeks (in the second trimester).\n        *   *3rd Visit*: Between 28 and 34 weeks (in the third trimester).\n        *   *4th Visit*: At 36 weeks and onwards until delivery.\n3.  **Physical and Obstetrical Examination**:\n    *   *Weight Monitoring*: Average weight gain should be 10–12 kg during a normal pregnancy (1 kg in 1st trimester, 5 kg each in 2nd and 3rd trimesters).\n    *   *Blood Pressure (BP)*: Checked at every visit to screen for pre-eclampsia (BP \\ge 140/90 mmHg).\n    *   *Abdominal Palpation*: Measuring fundal height to assess gestational age and fetal growth, and palpating for fetal lie, presentation, and position.\n    *   *Auscultation*: Checking the Fetal Heart Rate (FHR) (normal: 120–160 bpm).\n    *   *Pedal Edema*: Assessment for excessive, pathological swelling of the feet.\n4.  **Essential Laboratory Investigations**:\n    *   Hemoglobin (Hb) estimation to detect anemia (Hb < 11 g/dL).\n    *   Urine examination for albumin (marker of pre-eclampsia) and sugar (marker of gestational diabetes).\n    *   Blood grouping and Rh typing to manage Rh-negative pregnancies.\n    *   Screening for HIV, Syphilis (VDRL), Hepatitis B (HBsAg), and Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM using OGTT).\n5.  **Prophylaxis and Supplementation**:\n    *   **Iron-Folic Acid (IFA)**: One tablet (100 mg elemental iron + 0.5 mg folic acid) daily for a minimum of 180 days starting from the second trimester.\n    *   **Calcium Supplementation**: 1g daily from 14 weeks onwards to support fetal bone growth and prevent pre-eclampsia.\n    *   **Tetanus Toxoid (TT/Td)**: Two doses of Td vaccine (1st dose at registration, 2nd dose 4 weeks later; or a single booster dose if previously vaccinated within 3 years).\n6.  **Counseling and Birth Preparedness**:\n    *   Dietary advice (extra 350 kcal/day in pregnancy, rich in proteins and iron).\n    *   Personal hygiene, dental care, and avoidance of heavy physical labor.\n    *   Educating on danger signs (vaginal bleeding, severe headache, blurred vision, decreased fetal movements, swelling of face/hands) and planning for emergency transport.",
    "page": 27
