    "id": 264,
    "text": "Write short note on School health programme",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "SPM in Obs, Paeds, and Geriatrics",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### **School Health Programme**\n\n#### **1. Rationale**\nSchool children (aged 5–15 years) represent nearly **20–25%** of India's population. They are in a phase of rapid physical, mental, and social development, making them highly receptive to health education and susceptible to preventable illnesses. The **School Health Programme** is designed to promote, protect, and restore health at this crucial stage.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Components of the School Health Programme**\n\n1.  **Health Appraisal and Screenings**:\n    *   Conducted annually for all students and teachers by a visiting medical team.\n    *   Screening covers: nutritional deficiencies (anemia, Vitamin A and D deficiencies), dental caries, refractive errors (vision checkups), skin infections (scabies, ringworm), and hearing impairments.\n2.  **Remedial Measures and Referral Services**:\n    *   Establishing health rooms in schools. Minor ailments are treated on-site (e.g., distributing iron tablets, worm treatments, basic wound dressing).\n    *   Children with major issues (severe refractive errors, congenital heart disease, behavioral disorders) are referred to designated primary health centres or district hospitals.\n3.  **Prevention and Control of Communicable Diseases**:\n    *   Enforcing age-appropriate booster immunizations (e.g., Td vaccine at 10 and 16 years).\n    *   Proactive detection and temporary isolation of students with contagious diseases (chickenpox, conjunctivitis, mumps) to prevent school-wide outbreaks.\n4.  **School Mid-Day Meal Scheme (MDMS)**:\n    *   Provides one hot, freshly cooked meal daily to every child in government and government-aided primary and upper-primary schools.\n    *   *Primary Stage*: Provides a minimum of 450 calories and 12g of protein.\n    *   *Upper-Primary Stage*: Provides a minimum of 700 calories and 20g of protein.\n    *   Significantly combats childhood malnutrition and boosts school enrollment and retention.\n5.  **Sanitation and School Environment**:\n    *   Provision of clean, safe drinking water.\n    *   Separate, hygienic toilets for boys and girls to promote menstrual hygiene and reduce dropouts among adolescent girls.\n    *   Ensuring classroom design permits adequate cross-ventilation and natural daylighting (to reduce eye strain).\n6.  **Mental Health and Behavioral Counseling**:\n    *   Identifying learning disabilities (e.g., dyslexia), attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), juvenile delinquency risk, and providing guidance to combat scholastic backwardness and emotional stress.\n7.  **Health and Hygiene Education**:\n    *   Integrating curriculum on personal hygiene, regular handwashing, road safety, physical exercise, and tobacco/drug abuse prevention.",
