    "id": 289,
    "text": "Functions of Primary Health Centre.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Care of the Community",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### **Functions of a Primary Health Centre (PHC)**\n\n#### **1. Overview and Scale**\nA **Primary Health Centre (PHC)** is the first referral level facility in the rural health infrastructure, designed to provide comprehensive, integrated curative and preventive services. It is established at the scale of:\n*   **1 PHC per 30,000 population** in plain areas.\n*   **1 PHC per 20,000 population** in hilly, tribal, and backward areas.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Key Functions of a PHC (IPHS Guidelines)**\n\n1.  **Medical Care**:\n    *   Outpatient Department (OPD) services for daily clinical diagnosis and treatment.\n    *   Inpatient care (usually 4 to 6 beds) for observation and management of minor illnesses.\n    *   24-hour emergency services, including stabilization of trauma cases before referral.\n2.  **Maternal and Child Health (MCH) Care**:\n    *   Provision of antenatal checkups, screening for high-risk pregnancies, and postnatal care.\n    *   Conduction of safe institutional deliveries. Under IPHS, **Type B PHCs** function as 24/7 delivery points conducting $\\ge 20$ deliveries per month.\n3.  **Family Welfare Services**:\n    *   Counseling on modern contraceptive methods. Insertion of intrauterine devices (IUDs like CuT-380A), distribution of oral contraceptive pills and condoms, and referral for tubectomy/vasectomy.\n4.  **Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)**:\n    *   Maintenance of the vaccine cold chain using Ice-Lined Refrigerators (ILRs) and Deep Freezers.\n    *   Conduction of weekly immunization sessions at the PHC and peripheral Anganwadi Centres.\n5.  **Control of Communicable and Endemic Diseases**:\n    *   Active diagnosis and treatment of tuberculosis (functioning as a DOTS microscopy center), malaria (conducting blood smear examinations), leprosy, and water-borne outbreaks.\n6.  **Safe Drinking Water and Basic Sanitation**:\n    *   Regular sanitary inspection of local water sources and checking for proper chlorination to prevent water-borne epidemics.\n7.  **School Health Services**:\n    *   Periodic health screenings, checkups, and nutritional assessments of children in primary and secondary schools within its jurisdiction.\n8.  **Vital Statistics and Surveillance**:\n    *   Reporting and registration of births and deaths occurring in its area, and uploading epidemiological data to the Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP) portal.",
