    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### **Functions of the World Health Organization (WHO)**\n\n#### **1. Background**\nThe **World Health Organization (WHO)** is a specialized, non-political agency of the United Nations established on **7th April 1948** (commemorated annually as **World Health Day**), with its global headquarters in Geneva, Switzerland. Its primary constitutional objective is the attainment by all peoples of the highest possible level of health.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Key Functions of WHO**\nWHO functions as the global directing and coordinating authority on international health work through several key domains:\n\n1.  **Directing and Coordinating International Health Work**:\n    *   Drafting international sanitary regulations and coordinating global health policies.\n    *   Establishing health standards and guidelines (e.g., WHO Air Quality Guidelines, WHO Growth Charts).\n2.  **Prevention and Control of Epidemic and Endemic Diseases**:\n    *   Spearheading global eradication and elimination campaigns (e.g., historical eradication of Smallpox, ongoing Global Polio Eradication Initiative, and global strategies for Malaria, TB, and HIV/AIDS).\n    *   Maintaining the Global Outbreak Alert and Response Network (GOARN) for early disease warning.\n3.  **Strengthening of National Health Services**:\n    *   Providing technical assistance and expertise to member states to build resilient, primary health-care-based health systems, human resource training, and policy formulation.\n4.  **Maternal and Child Health (MCH)**:\n    *   Promoting safe motherhood practices, child survival strategies, sexual and reproductive health policies, and adolescent health.\n5.  **Development of Biomedical Research and Technical Standards**:\n    *   Standardizing diagnostic procedures, medical equipment, and biological substances (vaccine protocols).\n    *   Maintaining and publishing the **International Classification of Diseases (ICD)** for global mortality and morbidity reporting.\n6.  **Environmental Health & Sanitation**:\n    *   Formulating standards for safe drinking water, environmental sanitation, food safety, and monitoring occupational hazards.\n7.  **Emergency Humanitarian Assistance**:\n    *   Coordinating international medical relief and logistics during pandemics (e.g., COVID-19), natural disasters, and conflicts.",
    "page": 30
