    "text": "NATIONAL VECTOR BORNE DISEASE CONTROL PROGRAM(NVBDCP:) a) Strategies for malaria,Targets, Organisation, Indicators b) Urban malaria scheme c) Fever treatment depots, drug distribution centres.",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Programmes in India",
    "stars": 6,
    "answer": "### National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP)\n\nThe National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP) is the central nodal agency in India for the prevention, control, and elimination of six major vector-borne diseases (VBDs): **Malaria, Lymphatic Filariasis, Kala-azar, Dengue, Chikungunya, and Japanese Encephalitis (JE)**. \n\n---\n\n### Part A: Strategies for Malaria, Targets, Organisation, and Indicators\n\n#### 1. Targets (under the National Framework for Malaria Elimination - NFME 2016-2030):\n* **Eliminate Malaria nationally** (achieve zero indigenous malaria cases) by **2030**.\n* Maintain malaria-free status in areas where transmission has been interrupted.\n* **Intermediate Targets**: Eliminate malaria from Category 1 (low endemic) and Category 2 (moderate endemic) states by 2022, and Category 3 (high endemic) states by 2027.\n\n#### 2. Strategies for Malaria Control & Elimination:\nThe programme relies on a multi-pronged approach divided into three core pillars:\n\n##### I. Case Management (Early Diagnosis & Complete Treatment - EDCT):\n* **Diagnosis**:\n  * Sputum/Blood slide examination (thick and thin smears) at health facilities.\n  * Universal access to **Rapid Diagnostic Kits (RDTs)** at the community level through ASHAs for immediate diagnosis (differentiating between *P. vivax* and *P. falciparum*).\n* **Treatment Regimen**:\n  * **Plasmodium vivax**: Chloroquine (25 mg/kg body weight divided over 3 days) + **Primaquine** (0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days to prevent relapses). *Contraindicated in G6PD deficiency and pregnancy*.\n  * **Plasmodium falciparum**: **Artemisinin-based Combination Therapy (ACT)**. \n    * In most of India: **ACT-SP** (Artesunate + Sulfadoxine-Pyrimethamine) for 3 days + a single dose of **Primaquine** (0.75 mg/kg on Day 2).\n    * In Northeastern States (due to resistance): **ACT-AL** (Artemether + Lumefantrine) for 3 days + a single dose of Primaquine (0.75 mg/kg on Day 2).\n\n##### II. Integrated Vector Management (IVM):\n* **Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS)**: Conducted in high-risk areas (Annual Parasite Incidence > 2) using WHO-approved insecticides like DDT (50% WP), Synthetic Pyrethroids (Deltamethrin/Cyfluthrin), or Malathion.\n* **Long-Lasting Insecticidal Nets (LLINs)**: Free distribution of LLINs in highly endemic areas to provide physical protection and insecticidal knockdown effect.\n* **Biological Control**: Use of larvivorous fish (*Gambusia affinis* and *Poecilia reticulata*) in ornamental pools, wells, and slow-moving water channels.\n* **Environmental Management**: Source reduction (draining stagnant water, filling puddles) and weekly dry-day observance.\n\n##### III. Supportive Interventions:\n* **Information, Education, and Communication (IEC) / Behaviour Change Communication (BCC)**.\n* Capacity building of health staff and inter-sectoral coordination.\n\n#### 3. Organisation:\n* **National Level**: Directorate of NVBDCP (under Director General of Health Services, MoHFW, New Delhi), which provides policy guidelines, technical support, and budgetary resources.\n* **State Level**: State Vector Borne Disease Control Officer (SVBDCO) coordinating activities across districts.\n* **District Level**: District Malaria Officer (DMO) managing field operations, stock supply, and surveillance.\n* **Primary Health Centre (PHC) / Community Level**: Medical Officers, Health Assistants, Multi-Purpose Health Workers (MPWs), and **ASHAs** at the grassroots level.\n\n#### 4. Surveillance Indicators:\n* **Annual Parasite Incidence (API)**: The number of confirmed malaria cases per 1,000 population per year.\n  $$\\text{API} = \\frac{\\text{Total Confirmed Malaria Cases in a Year}}{\\text{Total Population under Surveillance}} \\times 1,000$$\n* **Annual Blood Examination Rate (ABER)**: Measures the index of surveillance activity. Target is **> 10%**.\n  $$\\text{ABER} = \\frac{\\text{Total Blood Smears Examined + RDTs done}}{\\text{Total Population}} \\times 100$$\n* **Slide Positivity Rate (SPR)**:\n  $$\\text{SPR} = \\frac{\\text{Total Slides Found Positive}}{\\text{Total Slides Examined}} \\times 100$$\n* **Slide Falciparum Rate (SFR)**: Measures the proportion of *P. falciparum* cases.\n  $$\\text{SFR} = \\frac{\\text{Total Slides Positive for P. falciparum}}{\\text{Total Slides Examined}} \\times 100$$\n\n---\n\n### Part B: Urban Malaria Scheme (UMS)\n\nLaunched in **1971**, the Urban Malaria Scheme covers 131 towns and cities in India. \n\n* **The Vector**: The primary urban vector is **Anopheles stephensi**, which breeds in clean artificial water collections (overhead tanks, cisterns, wells, coolers, construction sites, and containers).\n* **Control Strategies under UMS**:\n  * **Larval Source Reduction**: The main focus of UMS. Regulations to make overhead water tanks insect-proof. Weekly cleaning of coolers and domestic water containers (\"Dry Day\").\n  * **Chemical Larviciding**: Weekly application of larvicides like **Temephos (Abate)**, Pyriproxyfen, or larvicidal oil (MLO) to water bodies.\n  * **Biological Control**: Release of larvivorous fish in wells and large water tanks.\n  * **Space Spraying**: Outdoor fogging with Pyrethrum extract during outbreaks or high vector density periods.\n  * **Early Diagnosis and Treatment**: Provided through urban health posts, municipal hospitals, and private practitioners.\n\n---\n\n### Part C: Fever Treatment Depots (FTDs) and Drug Distribution Centres (DDCs)\n\nTo ensure immediate access to diagnostics and treatment in remote rural areas, the program decentralized services by setting up DDCs and FTDs.\n\n* **Drug Distribution Centres (DDCs)**:\n  * Established in endemic villages and managed by voluntary community workers, postmasters, or teachers.\n  * They are stocked with essential antimalarial drugs (Chloroquine and ACTs) to provide immediate treatment to presumptive malaria cases.\n* **Fever Treatment Depots (FTDs)**:\n  * In addition to storing and distributing antimalarial drugs, FTDs are authorized and trained to **collect blood smears** (thick and thin slides) and perform **Rapid Diagnostic Tests (RDTs)** on any patient presenting with fever.\n  * Managed closely by **ASHAs** or trained community health volunteers.\n* **Public Health Importance**: They minimize the crucial \"time lag\" between the onset of fever and the administration of radical treatment, preventing simple malaria cases from progressing to severe, life-threatening complications (like cerebral malaria) and reducing the reservoir of transmission in the community.",
