    "text": "a)NATIONAL LEPROSY ERADICATION PROGRAMME:  initiatives Targets, strategies, Organisation & Indicators b)Disability ,Prevention and Medical Rehabilitation in leprosy",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Programmes in India",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP)\n\nThe National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP) was launched by the Government of India in **1983** as a successor to the National Leprosy Control Programme of 1955. It is a 100% centrally sponsored scheme.\n\n---\n\n### Part A: Initiatives, Targets, Strategies, Organisation, and Indicators\n\n#### 1. Targets:\n* Achieve **Elimination of Leprosy** (Prevalence Rate < 1 case per 10,000 population) in all districts.\n* Reduce the rate of **Grade 2 Disability (G2D)** cases to less than 1 case per million population.\n* Reduce new child cases of leprosy to zero, signaling the interruption of ongoing transmission in the community.\n* Achieve zero stigma and discrimination against leprosy patients.\n\n#### 2. Key Initiatives:\n* **Leprosy Case Detection Campaign (LCDC)**: High-intensity, active house-to-house screening campaigns in high-endemic districts.\n* **Sparsh Leprosy Awareness Campaign (SLAC)**: Conducted annually on **30th January** (Anti-Leprosy Day / martyrdom day of Mahatma Gandhi) to reduce stigma and promote voluntary self-reporting.\n* **Nikusth Portal**: A web-based case notification and monitoring system for real-time tracking of leprosy patients and treatment outcomes.\n* **Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP)**: Administration of a **Single Dose of Rifampicin (SDR)** to all eligible household and social contacts of a newly diagnosed leprosy patient to prevent transmission.\n\n#### 3. Core Strategies:\n* **Decentralized Services**: Integration of leprosy services into the general health care delivery system (PHCs, CHCs, and District Hospitals).\n* **Early Detection**: Combined active surveillance (LCDC) and passive surveillance (self-reporting promoted through mass media).\n* **Standard Multi-Drug Therapy (MDT)**: Provided completely free of cost.\n  * **Paucibacillary (PB) Leprosy** (1 to 5 skin lesions; no bacilli on skin smear):\n    * **Drugs**: Rifampicin (600 mg once monthly, supervised) + Dapsone (100 mg daily, self-administered).\n    * **Duration**: **6 months** (to be completed within 9 months).\n  * **Multibacillary (MB) Leprosy** (More than 5 skin lesions; or positive skin smear):\n    * **Drugs**: Rifampicin (600 mg once monthly, supervised) + Clofazimine (300 mg once monthly supervised, and 50 mg daily self-administered) + Dapsone (100 mg daily, self-administered).\n    * **Duration**: **12 months** (to be completed within 18 months).\n  * *Note: Pediatric doses are adjusted according to body weight.*\n\n#### 4. Organisation:\n* **National Level**: Central Leprosy Division under the Directorate General of Health Services (DGHS), MoHFW.\n* **State Level**: State Leprosy Officer (SLO).\n* **District Level**: District Leprosy Officer (DLO) assisted by Leprosy Consultants and Non-Medical Supervisors (NMS).\n* **Primary Health Centre (PHC)**: Medical Officer, ANM, and Multi-Purpose Workers.\n* **Community Level**: **ASHAs** are highly incentivized for:\n  * Referral of suspected cases (early detection).\n  * Ensuring complete treatment adherence (treatment completion bonus).\n\n#### 5. Epidemiological Indicators:\n* **Prevalence Rate (PR)**: Measures the caseload in the population.\n  $$\\text{Prevalence Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Active Leprosy Cases on Record}}{\\text{Total Population}} \\times 10,000$$\n* **Annual New Case Detection Rate (ANCDR)**: Measures the incidence of disease.\n  $$\\text{ANCDR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of New Cases Detected during the Year}}{\\text{Total Population}} \\times 100,000$$\n* **Child Case Rate**: The percentage of children (< 15 years) among newly diagnosed cases (reflects recent, active transmission in the community).\n* **Grade 2 Disability (G2D) Rate**: The number of new cases presenting with visible deformities per million population.\n\n---\n\n### Part B: Disability Prevention and Medical Rehabilitation (DPMR) in Leprosy\n\nDPMR forms the **tertiary level of prevention** in NLEP, focusing on preserving the quality of life of patients and integrating them back into society.\n\n#### 1. Prevention of Disability (POD):\n* **Early Treatment of Lepra Reactions**: Lepra reactions (Type 1 and Type 2) can cause acute neuritis, leading to permanent nerve damage. Rapid management with anti-inflammatory drugs, corticosteroids (Prednisolone), and rest is essential.\n* **Care of Insensitive Hands, Feet, and Eyes**:\n  * Patients are taught **Self-Care Techniques** to manage anesthetic extremities:\n    * *Soak*: Soak feet in water daily for 20 minutes to soften the skin.\n    * *Scrape*: Gently scrape away hard calluses using a pumice stone.\n    * *Oil*: Apply oil (like coconut oil) on wet skin to lock in moisture.\n    * *Protect*: Always wear customized **Microcellular Rubber (MCR) Footwear** to prevent painless plantar ulcers.\n    * *Eye Care*: Frequent blinking, wearing protective sunglasses, and using artificial tears to prevent corneal dryness and exposure keratitis in facial nerve palsy (Lagophthalmos).\n\n#### 2. Medical Rehabilitation:\n* **Reconstructive Surgery (RCS)**:\n  * Surgical correction of established deformities such as claw hand, foot drop, lagophthalmos, and nasal collapse.\n  * NLEP provides financial assistance (Rs. 8,000 to Rs. 12,000) to patients undergoing RCS in recognized tertiary hospitals to compensate for loss of wages.\n* **Provision of Aids and Assistive Devices**:\n  * Free distribution of splints, crutches, wheel-chairs, and customized MCR footwear.\n\n#### 3. Socio-Economic Rehabilitation:\n* Providing vocational training to cured leprosy patients to make them financially self-reliant.\n* Coordinating with social welfare departments for disability pensions, housing, and combatting legal and social discrimination.",
