    "text": "NTEP( NATIONAL TUBERCULOSIS ELIMINATION PROGRAM) or previously RNTCP: objectives, components, Targets ,organization, laboratory network ,newer strategies,initiatives b) DOTS plus for MDR-TB                                                                            (10+5)",
    "type": "LAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Programmes in India",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP)\n\nThe Revised National TB Control Programme (RNTCP) was formally renamed the **National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP)** in **2020** to reflect India's commitment to eliminating Tuberculosis. The ambitious national goal is to **eliminate TB by 2025**, five years ahead of the global Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) target of 2030.\n\n---\n\n### Part A: Objectives, Components, Targets, Organisation, Laboratory Network, and Newer Strategies/Initiatives\n\n#### 1. Objectives of NTEP:\n* To achieve a rapid reduction in tuberculosis transmission, morbidity, and mortality.\n* To prevent the emergence of drug resistance.\n* To provide high-quality, free diagnostic and treatment services to all TB patients.\n\n#### 2. Targets (by 2025):\n* **Incidence Reduction**: 80% reduction in TB incidence rate (to less than **44 cases per 100,000** population).\n* **Mortality Reduction**: 90% reduction in TB mortality rate (to less than **3 deaths per 100,000** population).\n* **Financial Protection**: Achieve **Zero Catastrophic Cost** incurred by TB-affected households.\n\n#### 3. Strategic Pillars (under the National Strategic Plan - NSP 2017-2025):\nThe program is structured around four core pillars:\n* **DETECT**: Promptly find all active TB cases in both the public and private sectors, using rapid molecular diagnostics.\n* **TREAT**: Initiate all diagnosed TB patients on standard, weight-band based, daily fixed-dose combination (FDC) regimens with digital adherence support.\n* **PREVENT**: Scale up TB Preventive Therapy (TPT) among contacts of active cases, and expand BCG vaccination.\n* **BUILD**: Strengthen health systems, human resource capacities, community engagement, and IT platforms.\n\n#### 4. Organisation:\n* **National Level**: Central TB Division (CTD), Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, headed by the Deputy Director General (TB).\n* **State Level**: State TB Cell, managed by the State TB Officer (STO).\n* **District Level**: District TB Centre (DTC), managed by the District TB Officer (DTO).\n* **Sub-district Level (TB Unit - TU)**: Serves a population of approximately 1.5 to 2.5 lakhs (1 lakh in hilly/tribal areas). Managed by a Medical Officer-TB Control (MO-TC), Senior Treatment Supervisor (STS), and Senior TB Laboratory Supervisor (STLS).\n* **Peripheral Level**: Primary Health Centres (PHCs), Health & Wellness Centres, and community-based DOTS providers (ASHAs, Anganwadi workers, or local volunteers).\n\n#### 5. Laboratory Network:\n* **National and Supra-National Reference Laboratories (NRLs/SNRLs)**: Top-tier facilities for quality assurance, training, and research.\n* **Intermediate Reference Laboratories (IRLs)**: State-level labs capable of performing culture (liquid and solid) and Line Probe Assay (LPA).\n* **Molecular Testing Facilities**: **CBNAAT (GeneXpert)** and **Truenat** laboratories set up at the district and sub-district levels to provide rapid, highly sensitive molecular diagnosis and detect drug resistance.\n* **Designated Microscopy Centres (DMCs)**: Peripheral laboratories (at PHC/CHC level) performing Sputum Smear Microscopy (using Ziehl-Neelsen stain or LED Fluorescence microscopy).\n\n#### 6. Newer Strategies and Initiatives:\n* **Nikshay Portal**: A unified web-based IT platform that tracks the entire continuum of care for every TB patient, enabling real-time notification, adherence monitoring, and financial transfers.\n* **Nikshay Poshan Yojana (NPY)**: A Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) scheme providing **Rs. 500 per month** to all notified TB patients throughout their treatment period to support nutritional needs.\n* **Private Sector Engagement**: Partnership schemes like JEET (Joint Effort for Elimination of Tuberculosis) to encourage notification, diagnostic support, and free drug supply for patients seeking care in the private sector.\n* **Daily Regimen with Fixed-Dose Combinations (FDCs)**:\n  * Intermittent (thrice-weekly) treatment has been completely replaced with a **daily regimen** using FDCs based on weight bands.\n  * **Drug Sensitive TB (DS-TB) Regimen**:\n    * **Intensive Phase (IP - 2 months)**: Four drugs—**H**isoniazid, **R**ifampicin, **Z**azinamide, and **E**thambutol (HRZE).\n    * **Continuation Phase (CP - 4 months)**: Three drugs—**H**isoniazid, **R**ifampicin, and **E**thambutol (HRE).\n* **TB Preventive Therapy (TPT)**: Systematic screening and provision of preventive therapy (e.g., daily Isoniazid for 6 months or weekly Rifapentine + Isoniazid for 12 weeks) to all household contacts of pulmonary TB cases and People Living with HIV (PLHIV).\n\n---\n\n### Part B: DOTS-Plus for MDR-TB (Multi-Drug Resistant Tuberculosis)\n\nMDR-TB is defined as tuberculosis showing in-vitro resistance to at least **Isoniazid and Rifampicin**, the two most potent first-line drugs. NTEP manages these cases under the **Programmatic Management of Drug-Resistant TB (PMDT)** or **DOTS-Plus**.\n\n#### 1. Universal Drug Susceptibility Testing (UDST):\nThe cornerstone of DOTS-Plus is UDST, which mandates that **every diagnosed TB patient is screened for drug resistance** (specifically Rifampicin resistance) at the time of diagnosis using molecular tests (CBNAAT or Truenat) before starting therapy.\n\n#### 2. PMDT Treatment Regimens:\nDepending on drug susceptibility patterns, patients are placed on standardized, all-oral regimens:\n\n##### I. Shorter Oral MDR-TB Regimen (Duration: 9 to 11 months):\n* **Indications**: For patients with MDR/Rifampicin-Resistant TB who have not been pre-exposed to second-line drugs.\n* **Phases**:\n  * **Intensive Phase (4-6 months)**: 7 drugs daily—**Bedaquiline (Bdq)** (for 6 months), Levofloxacin (Lfx), Clofazimine (Cfz), Pyrazinamide (Z), Ethambutol (E), High-dose Isoniazid (H-hd), and Ethionamide (Eto).\n  * **Continuation Phase (5 months)**: 4 drugs daily—Levofloxacin (Lfx), Clofazimine (Cfz), Pyrazinamide (Z), and Ethambutol (E).\n\n##### II. All-Oral Longer M/XDR-TB Regimen (Duration: 18 to 20 months):\n* **Indications**: For patients with fluoroquinolone resistance (Pre-XDR TB) or those who fail the shorter regimen.\n* **Drug Composition**: Formulated using the WHO hierarchy of second-line drugs:\n  * **Group A (Highly Effective)**: **Levofloxacin / Moxifloxacin**, **Bedaquiline**, and **Linezolid**.\n  * **Group B (Second-line agents)**: **Clofazimine** and **Cycloserine**.\n  * **Group C (Add-on agents)**: Ethambutol, Delamanid, Pyrazinamide, Imipenem, or Amikacin.\n\n#### 3. Monitoring and Adherence:\n* **Clinical Monitoring**: Monthly clinical evaluations, monthly sputum smear and culture conversion assessments.\n* **Safety Monitoring**: Mandatory baseline and periodic ECGs (due to QTc prolongation risk associated with Bedaquiline and Clofazimine), complete blood counts (due to Linezolid-induced myelosuppression), and liver/renal function tests.\n* **Adherence**: Supported via digital tools (such as 99DOTS or MERM box) and supervised DOTS-Plus providers.",
