    "id": 339,
    "text": "Add a note on National health policy 2002.",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Planning and Management",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### National Health Policy (NHP) 2002\n\nThe National Health Policy (NHP) 2002 was formulated by the Government of India 19 years after the first National Health Policy (NHP 1983). The policy was designed to address the changing epidemiological landscape of the country, characterized by a **dual burden** of persistent communicable diseases and rapidly rising non-communicable lifestyle diseases (NCDs), alongside deep disparities in rural healthcare access.\n\n#### Core Objectives:\n* To achieve an acceptable standard of good health amongst the general population of the country.\n* To prioritize and increase access to primary healthcare services through the decentralized public health infrastructure, focusing specifically on the rural poor and underserved urban slum dwellers.\n\n#### Key Specific Targets of NHP 2002:\n1. **Eradication of Diseases**:\n   * Eradicate **Poliomyelitis** and **Yaws** by **2005** (Polio eradication was eventually certified in 2014; Yaws was eliminated).\n2. **Elimination of Diseases**:\n   * Eliminate **Leprosy** by **2005** (achieved at the national level in December 2005).\n   * Eliminate **Lymphatic Filariasis** by **2015**.\n   * Eliminate **Kala-azar** by **2010**.\n3. **Mortality and Morbidity Reduction**:\n   * Reduce mortality due to Malaria, Tuberculosis, and HIV/AIDS by **50%** by **2010**.\n   * Reduce the Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) to **30 per 1,000 live births** by **2010**.\n   * Reduce the Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR) to **100 per 100,000 live births** by **2010**.\n4. **Financial Targets**:\n   * Increase health sector expenditure by the Central Government to **2% of the Gross Domestic Product (GDP)**.\n   * Increase the state governments' budget allocation for health to **7% to 8%** of their total budgets.\n\n#### Salient Recommendations:\n* **Decentralization**: Strengthening primary healthcare infrastructure (PHCs/CHCs) and encouraging local self-government (Panchayati Raj institutions) to manage health funds.\n* **National Health Accounts**: Setting up a systematic database to track public and private health spending.\n* **Promotion of AYUSH**: Integrating traditional Indian systems of medicine (AYUSH) into the national health delivery system.\n* **Regulation of Private Sector**: Encouraging private sector participation in secondary and tertiary care while establishing strict quality regulatory standards.",
