    "text": "Add a note on niti aayog(3/5marks)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Planning and Management",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### NITI Aayog and its Role in the Health Sector\n\n**NITI Aayog** (National Institution for Transforming India) was established by the Government of India on **1st January 2015**, replacing the 65-year-old Planning Commission of India. \n\nUnlike the Planning Commission, which operated on a top-down, centralized planning model, NITI Aayog acts as the premier **policy think tank** of the government, promoting **Cooperative Federalism** by facilitating a bottom-up planning process that actively involves state governments.\n\n#### Structure:\n* **Chairperson**: The Prime Minister of India.\n* **Governing Council**: Consists of the Chief Ministers of all States and Lieutenant Governors of Union Territories.\n* **Executive Team**: Includes a Vice-Chairperson, Chief Executive Officer (CEO), and various full-time and part-time domain experts.\n\n#### Major Contributions and Role of NITI Aayog in the Health Sector:\n\n1. **The State Health Index (\"Healthy States, Progressive India\")**:\n   * Formulates and publishes an annual, comprehensive health index that ranks Indian states and union territories on three core domains: *Health Outcomes, Governance & Information, and Key Inputs/Processes*.\n   * This fosters a spirit of **competitive federalism**, encouraging states to outperform one another in improving public health indicators.\n\n2. **Universal Health Coverage and Ayushman Bharat**:\n   * Played a key structural and policy design role in conceptualizing and rolling out **Ayushman Bharat** (specifically the Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana - PMJAY), the world's largest government-funded health insurance scheme providing Rs. 5 Lakh coverage per family per year to the bottom 40% of the population.\n\n3. **Medical Education Reforms**:\n   * Drafted the landmark **National Medical Commission (NMC) Bill**, which successfully replaced the highly criticized Medical Council of India (MCI), introducing major reforms in medical education, seat licensing, and final exams (NEXT).\n\n4. **Public-Private Partnership (PPP) Guidelines**:\n   * Developed standard operating guidelines and models for PPP in healthcare, particularly for setting up NCD treatment wings, dialysis units, and advanced diagnostic centers at District Hospitals.\n\n5. **AYUSH Integration**:\n   * Actively promotes the mainstreaming and scientific integration of traditional systems of medicine (AYUSH) alongside modern allopathic systems.",
