    "text": "write about Twelfth five year plan (2012 - 2017)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Planning and Management",
    "stars": 1,
    "answer": "### Twelfth Five Year Plan (2012 - 2017): Health Sector\n\nThe **Twelfth Five Year Plan (2012-2017)** of India was structured under the broad national theme of **\"Faster, More Inclusive, and Sustainable Growth.\"** It was the last official Five Year Plan in India before the planning process was transitioned to NITI Aayog's strategic action agendas.\n\nIn the health sector, the Twelfth Plan marked a historic paradigm shift by adopting **Universal Health Coverage (UHC)** as its core vision, ensuring that all citizens have access to quality, essential health services without facing financial hardship.\n\n#### Key Health Targets of the Twelfth Plan:\n1. **Infant Mortality Rate (IMR)**: Reduce IMR to **25 per 1,000 live births**.\n2. **Maternal Mortality Ratio (MMR)**: Reduce MMR to **100 per 100,000 live births**.\n3. **Total Fertility Rate (TFR)**: Achieve and maintain a replacement-level TFR of **2.1**.\n4. **Child Nutrition**: Reduce the prevalence of under-nutrition in children aged 0-3 years to half of the baseline levels.\n5. **Gender Ratio**: Improve the child sex ratio (0-6 years) to **950 female births per 1,000 male births**.\n6. **Financial Protection**: Reduce out-of-pocket health expenditure by expanding public drug distribution systems.\n\n#### Core Strategies and Focus Areas:\n\n1. **Mainstreaming the National Health Mission (NHM)**:\n   * Fully integrated the successful National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) and the newly approved National Urban Health Mission (NUHM) to provide a seamless, unified continuum of primary healthcare.\n\n2. **Free Essential Drugs and Diagnostics Initiatives**:\n   * Launched dedicated schemes to provide free, quality-assured essential generic medicines and basic diagnostic tests in all public health facilities (PHCs, CHCs, DHs) to drastically cut out-of-pocket spending, which drives millions into poverty.\n\n3. **Strengthening Human Resources for Health (HRH)**:\n   * Funded the establishment of new government medical colleges, nursing institutions, and paramedical schools, focusing specifically on upgrading existing district hospitals in underserved and rural districts.\n\n4. **Nationwide Rollout of NCD Programmes**:\n   * Scaled up the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke (NPCDCS) across all districts, introducing systematic screening for chronic lifestyle diseases.\n\n5. **Decentralized District Health Planning**:\n   * Empowered districts to formulate localized \"District Health Action Plans\" customized to address their specific epidemiological and infrastructural needs.",
