    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Planning and Management",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### BHORE COMMITTEE (1943) — HEALTH SURVEY AND DEVELOPMENT COMMITTEE\n\n### BACKGROUND\nAppointed by the Government of India in 1943 under the chairmanship of Sir Joseph Bhore. Considered the **most comprehensive review of India's health system**. Its recommendations form the foundation of India's modern public health infrastructure.\n\n### KEY RECOMMENDATIONS\n1. **Integration of Preventive and Curative Services**: At all levels of healthcare — primary, secondary, and tertiary.\n2. **Concept of Primary Health Centre (PHC)**: First formal recommendation to establish PHCs to provide comprehensive healthcare to rural populations.\n3. **Short-term Goal**: 1 PHC for every 40,000 population.\n4. **Long-term Goal (30-year plan)**: 1 doctor and 1 nurse per 2,000 population; universal health coverage.\n5. **Social Orientation**: Doctors should have a \"social conscience\"; every medical institution should have a social/preventive medicine department.\n6. **Environmental Sanitation**: Emphasized water supply, sanitation, and food hygiene.\n7. **Medical Education Reform**: Advocated for a 3-month training in preventive medicine (\"social medicine\") for all undergraduates (this influenced the current MBBS Community Medicine posting).\n8. **District Health Organisation**: Each district should have a District Health Officer (DHO) responsible for preventive and curative services.\n\n### SIGNIFICANCE\n* Bhore Committee's recommendations directly led to the development of the **PHC System** in India post-independence, which remains the backbone of rural healthcare today.\n* Laid down the concept of \"Health for All\" decades before the Alma Ata Declaration (1978).",
    "page": 35
