    "text": "Recommendations of SHRIVASTAVA committee(1975)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Planning and Management",
    "stars": 4,
    "answer": "### SHRIVASTAVA COMMITTEE (1975) — HEALTH SERVICES AND MEDICAL EDUCATION\n\n### BACKGROUND\nFormally titled the \"Group on Medical Education and Support Manpower.\" Chaired by Dr. J.B. Shrivastava. Formed in response to the acute shortage of rural healthcare personnel and the maldistribution of doctors (most concentrated in cities).\n\n### KEY RECOMMENDATIONS\n1. **Community Health Worker (CHW) / Village Health Guide**: \n   * Recommended training **one community health worker from within each village** (a local, non-professional person) to provide basic health care at the village level.\n   * This was the predecessor to today's **ASHA (Accredited Social Health Activist)** worker.\n2. **Traditional Birth Attendants (TBAs / Dais)**: Recommended training of traditional dais for safe delivery practices.\n3. **Link Workers**: Intermediate health functionaries linking the CHW to the PHC system.\n4. **Rural Focus**: Emphasis on deploying health workers at the sub-centre and village level to address the urban-rural health disparity.\n5. **Preventive & Promotive Focus**: Shift emphasis from curative hospital-based medicine to preventive and community-based care.\n6. **Decentralisation of Health Services**: Services to be taken to the doorstep of rural communities.\n\n### SIGNIFICANCE\n* The CHW concept from this committee became the foundation of the **Community Health Worker Programme** and influenced the **ASHA scheme** under the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM, 2005).\n* Recognised that India cannot rely solely on doctors for rural healthcare — community-level, non-professional health workers are essential.",
