    "text": "Recommendations of KARTAR Singh committee (1973)",
    "type": "SAQ",
    "chapter": "Health Planning and Management",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### KARTAR SINGH COMMITTEE (1973) — MULTIPURPOSE WORKERS SCHEME\n\n### BACKGROUND\nFormally titled the \"Committee on Multipurpose Workers Under Health and Family Planning Programme.\" Chaired by Dr. Kartar Singh. Formed because separate workers existed for health (Health Visitor/ANM) and family planning, creating duplication and inefficiency.\n\n### KEY PROBLEM ADDRESSED\nAt the sub-centre level, **separate workers** handled health duties and family planning duties — this was wasteful and created confusion among the community. The Kartar Singh Committee addressed this fragmentation.\n\n### KEY RECOMMENDATIONS\n1. **Multipurpose Health Worker (MPW)**: Merge the roles of all health and family planning workers at the sub-centre into a single **Multipurpose Health Worker (Male and Female)**.\n2. **Sub-Centre Staffing**:\n   * 1 **MPW (Female)** = ANM (Auxiliary Nurse Midwife) — covers maternal and child health, family planning, nutrition.\n   * 1 **MPW (Male)** — covers environmental sanitation, disease surveillance, health education, family planning.\n3. **Norm**: 1 Sub-Centre for every **5,000 population** (3,000 in hilly/tribal areas).\n4. **PHC Coverage**: 1 PHC for every **30,000 population** (20,000 in hilly areas).\n5. **Supervisory Structure**: Lady Health Visitor (LHV) supervises 4 ANMs.\n\n### SIGNIFICANCE\n* Recommendations of this committee led to the **Multipurpose Worker Scheme (1977)**, which became the foundation of India's sub-centre system.\n* Sub-centres remain the first point of contact between the health system and rural communities, staffed by the MPW (ANM) as recommended.",
