    "chapter": "Health Planning and Management",
    "stars": 3,
    "answer": "### MUDALIAR COMMITTEE (1962) — HEALTH SURVEY AND PLANNING COMMITTEE\n\n### BACKGROUND\nFormally titled the \"Health Survey and Planning Committee.\" Chaired by Dr. A.L. Mudaliar. Formed to review the implementation of Bhore Committee recommendations after 15 years of independence.\n\n### CONTEXT & FINDINGS\n* The Bhore Committee's ambitious 30-year plan had NOT been fully implemented.\n* PHCs were established but were **understaffed and overcrowded** (1 PHC was serving populations far larger than recommended).\n* Quality of services at the district level was deteriorating.\n\n### KEY RECOMMENDATIONS\n1. **Strengthening of District Hospitals**: Improve equipment, staffing, and specialist services at District Hospitals (DHs).\n2. **Strengthening of CHCs (Community Health Centres)**: Establish CHCs at the block/taluk level as first-referral units.\n3. **Quality Over Quantity**: Recommended consolidating existing PHCs and improving their quality rather than expanding the network indiscriminately.\n4. **Upgraded PHC**: Each PHC should have at least **6 indoor beds**.\n5. **Medical Social Workers**: Each PHC should have trained medical social workers.\n6. **Central Health Services**: Recommended creation of an all-India health cadre (which led to Central Health Services [CHS]).\n7. **Specialised Hospitals**: Emphasis on developing mental hospitals, TB sanatoriums, and leprosy hospitals.\n\n### SIGNIFICANCE\n* Led to the strengthening of **CHCs** as functional referral units between PHC and District Hospital.\n* Recognised the urban bias in healthcare resource allocation and recommended more equitable distribution.",
